{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_51","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,100p' implementation_notes.md; git status --porcelain=v1 --untracked-files=all -- formal fpga tools bench test/cosim\"","aggregated_output":"# Shared six-round divider\n\nThe EX ALU now shares one unsigned restoring divider across DIV, DIVU, REM and REMU. It captures the forwarded operands, runs six rounds of six radix-2 steps, and transfers the result on the eighth EX cycle when MEM can advance. The divider registers the partial remainder and reuses a 36-bit shift register for the padded dividend and outgoing quotient bits. ALTOPS captures the existing stand-in result and uses the same handshake and latency.\n\nThe divider derives signed results from unsigned magnitudes. A zero divisor naturally leaves the dividend magnitude in the remainder and produces an all-ones quotient. Sign restoration preserves the original dividend for a zero-divisor remainder and skips quotient negation in that case. The 32-bit magnitude and result also preserve `INT_MIN / -1` and its zero remainder.\n\nFetch and decode hold while EX waits. When MEM advances during the divide, EX/MEM captures a bubble with inactive controls, letting older instructions retire. A divide captures its forwarded values for RVFI at issue and passes through EX/MEM once, where the usual bypass serves the next instruction. The core retains its single-channel RVFI interface.\n\nA guarded include brings the core-wide types into scope for the requested alphabetical Verilator glob. Fetch flush is driven independently of the load-use check to remove Verilator's apparent feedback path in VPI builds.\n\n## Local checks\n\n- Strict Verilator lint passed in hardware and ALTOPS modes.\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench JOBS=8 bash formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-channel ALTOPS checks with zero failures. The deep arithmetic suite was outside this run. Cocotb and cosim exercised the hardware arithmetic.\n- `make test TARGET=bench` passed all five cocotb runners. Divider coverage includes exact completion latency, delayed acceptance, consecutive requests, reset during a divide, and signed and unsigned edge cases. The pipeline regression checks retirement order and forwarding through a stalled older load.\n- `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench` passed all five ELFs. Selftest and three random programs matched the ISS with and without bus stalls. CoreMark passed all CRC checks with both stalls active.\n\n## Gowin and CoreMark\n\n| Metric | Baseline | Shared divider |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Marker-bracketed cycles | 4,491,485 | 4,491,485 |\n| Median Fmax, MHz | 5.442 | 42.123 |\n| CoreMark iterations per second | 12.12 | 93.78 |\n| Gowin logic cells | 12,312 | 2,773 |\n| Gowin registers | 460 | 565 |\n\n`NRET=1 make fpga TARGET=bench` measured 41.866, 42.123 and 42.123 MHz across the three placement options. Equal bracketed cycles yield 7.74× baseline throughput. The option-0 worst setup path runs between two divider remainder registers, with 29.180 ns of data arrival time. All three options place their worst setup path within the divider. The baseline 199.837 ns forwarding-to-data-memory path is no longer the limiting path. [Gowin option-0 report](cores/bench/generated/gowin_p0/impl/pnr/core_bench.tr)\n\nThe eight-cycle EX occupancy adds seven cycles per divide without backpressure, so workloads that frequently divide can lose cycles relative to the prior single-cycle execution. The six-step divider round now limits Fmax.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
