{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_36","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"test -s implementation_notes.md && rg -n 'Formal:|DONE \\\\(FAIL|DONE \\\\(ERROR' formal/last_run-wuehS3BMQ.log | tail -8\nsed -n '1,38p' implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_36","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"test -s implementation_notes.md && rg -n 'Formal:|DONE \\\\(FAIL|DONE \\\\(ERROR' formal/last_run-wuehS3BMQ.log | tail -8\nsed -n '1,38p' implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Bench implementation notes\n\nEX/MEM captures the full forwarded `rs1 + imm` sum in `effective_addr` and holds it during data backpressure. MEM uses that field for the bus address, lane masks, alignment checks, load extraction, and the word-aligned RVFI address. JALR clears bit zero on its target path. The existing ALU result still feeds forwarding and writeback.\n\nThe divider decodes the leading two dividend bits in the first update and registers the 34-bit `3D` on the same edge. Each of the next six updates runs two radix-4 steps with parallel 35-bit trials against `D`, `2D`, and `3D`, then one radix-2 step. Each radix-4 step selects its remainder with one-hot enables derived from the ordered borrow flags. The hardware and ALTOPS builds share the launch, seven updates, held-valid result, reset, and consume schedule. The datapath follows the proposed design. Directed cocotb cases add coverage for all first-update digit inputs, wide divisor multiples, and an odd-byte store under backpressure followed by loads and alignment traps.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator `-Wall` lint passed for hardware and ALTOPS builds.\n- `formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-retire ALTOPS checks on the first run. The wrapper matched `nret: 1` in `cores/bench/core.yaml`.\n- All five cocotb suites passed. A combinational SAT check established equivalence between each new two-bit step and two original restoring steps over the reachable remainder bounds.\n- Reference co-simulation passed selftest and all three random programs, each with and without bus stalls. The fitness harness validated all CoreMark CRCs with both stalls enabled and counted 4,191,341 marker-bracketed cycles for 10 iterations.\n\n## Timing and fitness\n\nGowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 measured 52.133, 53.281, and 53.281 MHz. Option 0 used 3,386 logic cells and 725 registers. At the 53.281 MHz median, CoreMark measured 127.12 iter/s, 11.45% above the 114.06 iter/s champion and above the 4.6% acceptance margin.\n\nThe measured median is 2.282 MHz below the scratch prototype. The longest setup path in all three placements runs from the register file to the EX/MEM ALU result register. The divider recurrence is close behind in both median placements.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
