{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"git log -6 --oneline && sed -n '1,130p' cores/bench/LESSONS.md\"","aggregated_output":"7386e08 log: hyp-20261119-003-r3s2 regression\n8d32941 log: hyp-20261119-002-r3s1 regression\nad07032 log: hyp-20261119-001-r3s0 improvement\n49ed678 hyp-20261119-001-r3s0: Shorten EX arithmetic paths to the divider and data memory (+11.4%)\nf82032a log: hyp-20261119-003-r2s2 improvement\n4dcf5ce log: hyp-20261119-002-r2s1 regression\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r1s0 (regression, +673.76%): Replacing combinational DIV/REM with a shared, registered restoring divider moved the critical path from EX-to-memory logic into the six-step divider round, raising median Gowin Fmax from 5.442 to 42.123 MHz while the divide-free CoreMark window avoided the extra EX cycles.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r1s2 (regression, +8.09%): With combinational DIV/REM in EX still limiting the clock to 5.5 MHz, the 16-entry tagged BTB's 6.1% CoreMark cycle savings from reusing taken branch and jump targets under bus stalls were too small to close the throughput gap to the 47.0 MHz registered-divider variant.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r2s0 (regression, +4.61%): Squashing decode through the ID/EX valid bit cut the EX redirect feedback through IF and hazard logic into the ID/EX payload, exposing the five-step divider update as the Gowin timing limit and leaving a placement-sensitive CoreMark gain at 49.128 MHz median Fmax with unchanged cycle count.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-002-r2s1 (regression, +6.19%): A one-entry fall-through fetch slot cut CoreMark cycles by 3.07% by saving ready imem responses during decode holds, while the round's BTB won at a lower median Fmax by avoiding taken-branch and jump bubbles and cutting cycles by 6.68%.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r2s2 (improvement, +9.09%): To pair the BTB's CoreMark cycle savings under bus stalls with a shorter recovery path, precompute full-width direct targets in ID, compare the fetch PC against target and fall-through in parallel with branch resolution, and redirect fetch with a validity-only ID/EX squash.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r3s0 (improvement, +11.45%): Replacing five serial restoring steps with two radix-4 steps using parallel divisor trials and one radix-2 step shortened the divider recurrence, and registering the unmasked JALR sum for load/store addresses bypassed the ALU result mux, together raising median Gowin Fmax from 47.807 to 53.281 MHz and shifting the critical path to the register-file-to-EX/MEM ALU result path.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-002-r3s1 (regression, +7.44%): MEM-to-EX load forwarding removed load-use bubbles and cut CoreMark cycles by 4.85%, while registering the full JALR adder sum for load/store addresses kept the readback path out of the ALU result mux and restored divider-limited timing, leaving 122.55 iter/s behind the round winner's 127.12 iter/s from a shorter divider recurrence.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r3s2 (regression, +8.72%): A four-entry fall-through FIFO let the BTB fetch across predicted transfers while decode or EX was held and let decode issue during imem stalls, cutting CoreMark cycles by 5.54% and reaching 124.01 iter/s at a divider-limited 49.095 MHz median Fmax, short of the round winner's 127.12 iter/s.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
