{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_23", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d.get('id') in ('hyp-20261119-001-r8s0','hyp-20261119-001-r7s0','hyp-20261119-003-r8s2'):\n  print(d.get('id'))\n  for key in ('implementation_notes', 'fpga'):\n   print(d.get(key))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261119-001-r7s0\n\nNone\nhyp-20261119-001-r8s0\n# Implementation notes\n\nEX decodes the load width into a two-bit byte index and registers it with the existing EX/MEM load controls. Byte loads use both low address bits, halfword loads use address bit 1 followed by a zero, and word loads select lane 0. MEM selects the low byte once from the raw response and applies each operand's forwarding enable and the writeback load select after the mux. The existing upper-byte and sign masks, misalignment checks, and pipeline holds still qualify the corresponding results.\n\nThe datapath follows the hypothesis. I used an explicit width case because it produced the fastest median in local Gowin measurements. Placement option 0 reached 68.729 MHz and was limited by the fetch replay path. The divider limited options 1 and 2, both at 75.894 MHz, giving a 75.894 MHz median.\n\n- Verilator lint and all five cocotb suites passed. The pipeline tests cover signed and unsigned subword lanes, both forwarding operands, zero load-use bubbles on ready responses, and stalls.\n- RVFI cosim passed selftest and all three random programs with both buses ready and with both stall streams enabled. CoreMark matched the canonical CRCs and completed 10 iterations in 3,337,377 marker-bracketed cycles.\n- The measured Fmax and CoreMark cycle count yield 227.41 iterations/s, up 6.86% from the 212.81 iterations/s champion.\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-channel ALTOPS checks on the final RTL, including register consistency. The run used an isolated scratch copy of the standard harness.\n\nNone\nhyp-20261119-003-r8s2\n# Implementation notes\n\n`cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv` replaces the 32-word countdown with an arm bit. An accepted taken BTB prediction or a registered taken recovery sets the bit. Sequential fetches and fall-through recovery retain it. Reset and advancing FENCE.I clear the bit and the validity bitmap with priority over arming and filling.\n\nThe 1,024-word direct-mapped RAM reads `PC[11:2]` on the edge that captures the ordinary next fetch PC. It stores the full `PC[19:12]` tag alongside the instruction. A full ten-bit comparison between the sampled read and fill indices masks same-edge collisions regardless of RAM mode.\n\nRaw imem readiness selects both fetch readiness and the instruction payload. The bus acceptance term `!fetch_parked || squash_candidate` feeds the readiness mux and helps qualify fills without a replay lookup. Each accepted ready word refreshes its entry while armed or on a taken correction. Replay requires an exact valid tag at an aligned address below 1 MiB. A recovery presents the full corrected PC and waits for imem readiness in the correction cycle.\n\nThe RTL follows the proposed architecture. The pipeline tests cover 40 sequential replay words, refresh from a ready response, every tag bit, simultaneous read and fill across the three widened index bits, a collision at a high index, and FENCE.I refetch, clear priority, and rearm.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed with `-Wall`.\n- `make test TARGET=bench` passed all five cocotb suites, including 49 pipeline cases.\n- `formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-retire ALTOPS checks on the final RTL. No formal fixes were needed.\n- `make cosim TARGET=bench NRET=1` passed RVFI comparison for selftest and three random programs with and without bus stalls, plus stalled CoreMark CRC validation. The local build used explicit `NRET=1` to match `core.yaml`.\n- `make fpga TARGET=bench NRET=1` reproduced all three Gowin results at 71.145, 74.271, and 74.271 MHz. The median was 74.271 MHz. Option 0 used 5,249 logic cells, 1,935 registers, and seven BSRAMs. The worst setup paths crossed data RAM into the EX multiply or shift result registers.\n- CoreMark completed in 3,192,109 marker-bracketed cycles with all expected CRCs and the UART validation message. The measured fitness was 232.67 iterations/s, up 9.33% from 212.81 iterations/s.\n\nNone\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
