ncurrent agents of a run share it through\n# their worktrees' symlink). The name used to be \"$CORE_NAME-$$\", but an\n# agent's sandboxed shell runs in its own PID namespace where $$ is 2 or 3\n# for every agent, so siblings wrote into one dir and wiped each other's\n# checks mid-SBY (V2 incident 03). mktemp gives a unique name instead.\n# FORMAL_WORK_ID pins the suffix so tools/eval/formal.py can remove exactly\n# its own dir afterwards. genchecks.py derives @core\n…[44779 of 47851 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:5f1dfc710934720e]…\nrence with and without bus stalls. CoreMark passed all UART and CRC checks under both stalls and ran 4,215,937 bracketed cycles, down 6.13% from the supplied baseline.\\n- Gowin measured 5.179, 5.521, and 5.521 MHz for placement options 0, 1, and 2. The 5.521 MHz median gives 13.096 CoreMark iterations per second, up 8.05% from the supplied 12.12 baseline.\\n\\n## Timing and area\\n\\nThe BTB adds a tag check and target mux to the fetch PC path. The worst reported paths still traverse the EX ALU, with placement option 0 reporting 12,911 logic cells, 479 registers, and 17 LUT-RAM cells for the timed design.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-19T14:33:35.953081Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r1s2 (regression, +8.09%): With combinational DIV/REM in EX still limiting the clock to 5.5 MHz, the 16-entry tagged BTB's 6.1% CoreMark cycle savings from reusing taken branch and jump targets under bus stalls were too small to close the throughput gap to the 47.0 MHz registered-divider variant.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
