{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_52","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"git diff --check; git status --short --ignored; sed -n '1,120p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n!! .agent.last\n!! .agent.log\n!! cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\n!! formal/riscv-formal\n# One-entry fall-through fetch slot\n\n## Implementation\n\n- IF holds a 32-bit instruction, its full PC, and a valid bit. An empty slot bypasses a ready instruction to decode or stores it when issue is blocked. A full slot can issue with imem unready and can refill on the same edge as issue. The sequential fetch PC advances on response acceptance.\n- The hazard unit derives issue permission from EX availability and a valid EX load's dependency on the selected head. ID/EX holds during a back-end stall and otherwise captures the decoded payload. Issue and squash qualify the ID/EX valid bit.\n- EX redirects on an advancing, valid taken branch or jump with an aligned target. Branch and JAL targets share the PC-relative sum. JALR uses the forwarded base and clears bit zero. A redirect overrides the sequential fetch PC and clears the slot and younger ID/EX validity on the same edge. An older dmem hold defers that edge.\n- EX/MEM clears the entire control bundle on invalid entries and unfinished divides, suppressing memory requests and forwarding. MEM/WB retains the older result for forwarding during a dmem hold and clears its single-channel RVFI retire valid.\n- The directed tests cover empty-slot bypass, load-use capture, full-slot refill, and queued issue during an imem stall. A forwarded JALR following a stalled store exercises redirect with imem both ready and unready, next-cycle target selection, wrong-path store suppression, and exact RVFI order.\n\n## Verification\n\n- The requested Verilator lint command and `make lint TARGET=bench` passed without warnings.\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-retire ALTOPS checks on the first attempt.\n- `make test TARGET=bench` passed all five cocotb runners. `make cosim TARGET=bench` with `NRET=1` matched the RVFI reference for selftest and all three random programs with and without bus stalls. Formal and cosim used staged copies of the existing harness files under `$TMPDIR`.\n- `make fpga TARGET=bench` with `NRET=1` validated CoreMark's UART and all five canonical CRCs with both bus stalls. The markers bracketed 4,353,657 cycles for 10 iterations, matching the prototype and improving cycle count by 3.07% from 4,491,485.\n- `make timing TARGET=bench` and the FPGA run measured 46.729, 48.337, and 48.337 MHz for Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2. The 48.337 MHz median clears the 47.616 MHz cutoff and is 0.781 MHz below the prototype median. The measured fitness is 111.03 iterations/s, up 6.18% from 104.56.\n\n## Timing and tradeoffs\n\n- The option 0 worst path has 26.695 ns reported arrival time and 16 cells from the register file through EX to data memory. The existing divider appears among the next worst paths. The timed build uses 2,819 logic cells and 627 registers, an increase of 65 registers over the champion.\n- The raw source-field load-use comparison can conservatively delay an instruction whose immediate bits match the load destination. This is the same dependency criterion used by the champion.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
