(imm >> 11) & 1\n    b1912  = (imm >> 12) & 0xFF\n    return (b20 << 31) | (b101 << 21) | (b11 << 20) | (b1912 << 12) \\\n         | ((rd & 0x1F) << 7) | (opcode & 0x7F)\n\n\ndef ADDI(rd, rs1, imm): return _i(imm & 0xFFF, rs1, 0b000, rd, 0b0010011)\ndef ADD (rd, rs1, rs2): return _r(0, rs2, rs1, 0b000, rd, 0b0110011)\ndef SUB (rd, rs1, r\n…[16260 of 19332 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:2b64313401d840b3]…\ne low-byte mux before per-destination load gating shortened the RAM-to-shifter forwarding path and exposed a divider-limited 75.894 MHz Gowin median, with unchanged CoreMark cycles leaving throughput behind the round winner's stall-reducing fetch replay.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-002-r8s1 (regression, +7.91%): Direct formatted-load forwarding to bytewise BEQ/BNE comparators kept all 135,320 immediate load-dependent CoreMark branches bubble-free, while replaying pointer loads and other consumers with MEM/WB forwarding cut the RAM-to-address path at a cycle cost that left throughput behind the round winner's larger instruction replay store.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r8s2 (improvement, +9.33%): Keeping BTB-armed replay eligible until reset or FENCE.I and growing the direct-mapped store from 128 to 1,024 words cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 4.35% by avoiding window expiry and reducing index conflicts, while deriving fills directly from accepted imem responses kept replay lookup off the RAM write-enable path.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
