{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_62", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,185p' cores/bench/LESSONS.md; rg --files cores/bench/experiments\"", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r1s0 (regression, +673.76%): Replacing combinational DIV/REM with a shared, registered restoring divider moved the critical path from EX-to-memory logic into the six-step divider round, raising median Gowin Fmax from 5.442 to 42.123 MHz while the divide-free CoreMark window avoided the extra EX cycles.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r1s2 (regression, +8.09%): With combinational DIV/REM in EX still limiting the clock to 5.5 MHz, the 16-entry tagged BTB's 6.1% CoreMark cycle savings from reusing taken branch and jump targets under bus stalls were too small to close the throughput gap to the 47.0 MHz registered-divider variant.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r2s0 (regression, +4.61%): Squashing decode through the ID/EX valid bit cut the EX redirect feedback through IF and hazard logic into the ID/EX payload, exposing the five-step divider update as the Gowin timing limit and leaving a placement-sensitive CoreMark gain at 49.128 MHz median Fmax with unchanged cycle count.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-002-r2s1 (regression, +6.19%): A one-entry fall-through fetch slot cut CoreMark cycles by 3.07% by saving ready imem responses during decode holds, while the round's BTB won at a lower median Fmax by avoiding taken-branch and jump bubbles and cutting cycles by 6.68%.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r2s2 (improvement, +9.09%): To pair the BTB's CoreMark cycle savings under bus stalls with a shorter recovery path, precompute full-width direct targets in ID, compare the fetch PC against target and fall-through in parallel with branch resolution, and redirect fetch with a validity-only ID/EX squash.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r3s0 (improvement, +11.45%): Replacing five serial restoring steps with two radix-4 steps using parallel divisor trials and one radix-2 step shortened the divider recurrence, and registering the unmasked JALR sum for load/store addresses bypassed the ALU result mux, together raising median Gowin Fmax from 47.807 to 53.281 MHz and shifting the critical path to the register-file-\n…[6496 of 9568 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:f3d01e44059af825]…\n the load with a final one-hot OR raised median Gowin Fmax to 75.702 MHz at unchanged CoreMark cycles, with the RAM-to-branch-training path and nearly tied multiply and shift paths keeping the design below the 77.69 MHz promotion floor.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-002-r9s1 (improvement, +7.93%): Ready-LW address and formatted BEQ/BNE bypasses kept CoreMark's dominant load-use pairs bubble-free, while independent redirect acceptance and precomputing the ordinary fetch successor for each BTB training outcome let registered branch resolution feed synchronous replay RAM through a final address select.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r9s2 (regression, -7.43%): Packing validity into a scrubbed 4K-word BTB-armed replay RAM saved 1,801 logic cells and used seven more BSRAMs, with the 0.28% stalled CoreMark cycle savings too small to offset the median Gowin Fmax drop from 74.271 to 68.560 MHz while the data RAM path into the EX shifter limited timing.\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\ncores/bench/experiments/progress.png\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
