{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_38","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"diff -qr cores/bench/rtl '/srv/hwebench/tmp/7cb5c576/bc4638ae/bench-btb-Ykc6JBjf'/cores/bench/rtl && cmp cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py '/srv/hwebench/tmp/7cb5c576/bc4638ae/bench-btb-Ykc6JBjf'/cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py && sed -n '1,200p' implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"# 16-entry tagged branch target buffer\n\n## Implementation\n\n- IF indexes the BTB with fetch PC bits [5:2] and checks the [19:6] tag for aligned PCs below 1 MiB. Conditional branches allocate on a taken miss at weakly taken and train a saturating two-bit counter on a hit. JAL and JALR cache the last representable target at strongly taken. Only valid bits require table reset.\n- IF selects the successor from the stored entry when ID accepts the instruction. A same-index EX update supplies the captured counter and tag match for training that instruction on its own EX advance. The captured state holds across data stalls.\n- EX compares the registered fetch PC with the full resolved successor on every valid advance. A mismatch squashes the younger instruction and redirects even with imem unready. An older data-memory hold defers both recovery and training. RVFI reports the same resolved successor, including PC+4 on a misaligned target trap.\n- EX sends compact source and target word addresses after checking BTB eligibility. A taken target outside the BTB range uses full-width recovery and leaves the previous entry available. JALR clears target bit zero before alignment checking or training.\n- The guarded include in `core.sv` makes the requested alphabetical RTL glob work with Verilator. Bench uses the single-retirement channel defined in its `core.yaml`.\n\n## Validation\n\n- The requested Verilator lint command and `make lint TARGET=bench` passed without warnings.\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-retirement ALTOPS checks on the first run.\n- `make test TARGET=bench` passed all five cocotb runners, including directed cases for adjacent training, index collisions, stale predictions, and JALR recovery with simultaneous bus backpressure and a later misaligned target.\n- `make cosim TARGET=bench` passed with `NRET=1`. Selftest and three random programs matched the RVFI reference with and without bus stalls. CoreMark passed all UART and CRC checks under both stalls and ran 4,215,937 bracketed cycles, down 6.13% from the supplied baseline.\n- Gowin measured 5.179, 5.521, and 5.521 MHz for placement options 0, 1, and 2. The 5.521 MHz median gives 13.096 CoreMark iterations per second, up 8.05% from the supplied 12.12 baseline.\n\n## Timing and area\n\nThe BTB adds a tag check and target mux to the fetch PC path. The worst reported paths still traverse the EX ALU, with placement option 0 reporting 12,911 logic cells, 479 registers, and 17 LUT-RAM cells for the timed design.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
