{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_39","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"git diff --check && git status --short --untracked-files=all && diff -qr cores/bench/rtl '/srv/hwebench/tmp/7cb5c576/8c524813/bench-btb.68preuep/cores/bench/rtl' && sed -n '1,140p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_39","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"git diff --check && git status --short --untracked-files=all && diff -qr cores/bench/rtl '/srv/hwebench/tmp/7cb5c576/8c524813/bench-btb.68preuep/cores/bench/rtl' && sed -n '1,140p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/mem_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n# BTB and validity-only recovery\n\nFetch uses a single lookup in a 32-entry direct-mapped BTB. Tag and word target share a RAM array, and the direction counters have a separate write enable. Reset clears the valid bits. Same-index EX updates bypass the counter and recompute the training hit from the update's source tag. Fetch can still select its successor from the pre-write entry.\n\nID captures PC+4 and computes the full B/J target from the raw instruction. EX checks the registered fetch PC against both possible successors and resolves JALR from the forwarded base and sign-extended immediate. EX uses the resolved full-width successor for recovery and RVFI, including fall-through on an alignment trap. The raw instruction feeds decode and load-use detection. A redirect squashes ID/EX by clearing its valid bit. Bus and divider holds preserve the older pipeline state, and EX/MEM bubbles have their side-effect and forwarding controls cleared.\n\nThe implementation removes the unused branch outcome and target fields from EX/MEM. An aligned conditional hit can train its direction counter with an out-of-range taken target. Its target entry is only refreshed when the new target fits in the BTB.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed with `-Wall`.\n- Local `formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-retire ALTOPS checks on the first run.\n- All five cocotb runners passed. The 19 pipeline cases include six new cases covering taken and untaken recovery, same-PC and conflicting-tag updates, JALR retargeting and misalignment, a stale taken prediction on a load, and a stalled store with imem still unready on the recovery cycle. They check RVFI order and exactly-once stores where applicable.\n- Reference cosim passed for selftest and all three random programs with and without bus stalls. CoreMark passed with both stalls enabled and all five canonical CRCs.\n- CoreMark took 4,191,341 bracketed cycles for 10 iterations, 6.68% fewer than the champion's 4,491,485 cycles and equal to the scratch prototype's count.\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 measured 47.375, 47.807 and 47.807 MHz. The 47.807 MHz median gives 114.06 CoreMark iterations per second, 9.09% above the 104.56 iter/s champion and above the 4.6% acceptance threshold. Gowin reported 26 LUT-RAM cells.\n\n## Timing concern\n\nThe two median placements report a 31-level critical path through the registered divider. That path limits the measured median below the scratch prototype's 49.426 MHz.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
