{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_97","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 -c 'from pathlib import Path; import xml.etree.ElementTree as ET; cases=[case for f in Path(\\\"cores/bench/test\\\").glob(\\\"*.result.xml\\\") for case in ET.parse(f).iter(\\\"testcase\\\")]; assert len(cases)==146 and all(case.find(\\\"failure\\\") is None and case.find(\\\"error\\\") is None and case.find(\\\"skipped\\\") is None for case in cases); print(f\\\"{len(cases)} tests passed\\\")' && sed -n '1,100p' implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"146 tests passed\n# Synchronous committed BTB lookup\n\nThe fetch PC, 32-entry BTB read, and replay read use the same selected next-PC index on each edge. The registered target is eligible for prediction when its full `PC[19:7]` tag matches and the registered PC passes the alignment and 1 MiB range checks. Validity and the two-bit direction counter come from committed state. Gowin maps the synchronous BTB array into one additional block RAM.\n\nAn aligned, in-range taken correction can write a target once per EX advance. On these cycles the read index comes from the recovery target or its sequential successor, so IF compares those indices directly for the sampled write collision. The next beat uses the captured write's tag and target on a collision. Reset clears validity and the collision flag.\n\nEX registers the source tag hit with the training record. Matching entries train with saturating counters. New branches initialize weakly taken and new jumps initialize strongly taken. IF keeps an asynchronous tag-only lookup for corrected instructions. The IF-to-ID and ID/EX metadata paths still forward pending writes and qualify replacements by tag. Ordinary next-PC selection uses committed state throughout.\n\nThe RTL retains full-width EX successor validation, last-word parking, FENCE.I replay invalidation, and single-channel RVFI retirement. The pipeline suite covers replacement collisions across all 13 tag bits, adjacent conditional branches, self-loops, JALR retargeting, misaligned targets, and corrections under instruction and data bus holds. A directed test also verifies that a correctly fetched taken successor leaves a different same-index entry and its direction counter intact.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint and all 146 cocotb cases across five runners passed.\n- Co-simulation passed on selftest with and without stalls. Stalled CoreMark completed in 3,202,739 bracketed cycles with the canonical CRCs and `Correct operation validated.`\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 checks with the standard single-channel wrapper and ALTOPS checks. No formal fix attempts were needed.\n- The selected block-RAM design uses the original one-hot ordinary PC selector. The Gowin flow measured 78.949, 81.195, and 81.195 MHz across placement options 0, 1, and 2. The divider limited all three placements. The 81.195 MHz median is 6.4% below the 86.757 MHz champion at the same CoreMark cycle count.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
