{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_98","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"git diff --check; git diff --numstat; sed -n '1,160p' implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"70\t8\tcores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n54\t0\tcores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n# Page-split JALR target and prediction validation\n\nEX now shares a 13-bit unsigned offset sum between JALR target generation and the ordinary fetch match. It compares the forwarded base page with the candidate page and its two neighbors, then uses the immediate sign to select a page match for each possible carry. The registered low match includes the parked-fetch qualifier and requires an even candidate address. Recovery selects the registered page match using the registered carry. Direct and fall-through comparisons still register in EX.\n\nThe full target selects the base, incremented, or decremented page and clears bit zero. The page arithmetic wraps at 20 bits. The target supplies recovery, alignment, the BTB payload, and RVFI. The BTB range predicate is computed from the possible pages in parallel so that eligibility can resolve without waiting for the assembled target. Its boundary checks cover both directions of wraparound. Same-cycle corrected fetch acceptance and predictor and replay update rules retain their existing behavior.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint and all 134 cocotb cases passed. The new pipeline cases exercise both directions of RV32 wraparound, a correct predicted page crossing, and a stale predicted page with the correct low offset.\n- A SAT check of the registered EX outputs proved target, target match, fall-through match, aligned next PC, and BTB target and eligibility equivalent to the full-width reference for arbitrary bases, signed immediates, candidate PCs, and parked state.\n- The unchanged single-channel `formal/run_all.sh` suite has passed 52 checks. `reg_ch0` is still running, with no counterexample reported and no fixes attempted.\n- Co-simulation passed selftest with and without memory stalls. Stalled CoreMark validated the canonical CRCs and completed 10 iterations in **3,202,739 bracketed cycles**.\n\n## Gowin timing\n\n| Placement option | Fmax | Limiting path |\n| --- | ---: | --- |\n| 0 | 75.959 MHz | Divider final result into EX/MEM |\n| 1 | 79.668 MHz | Divider partial-remainder feedback |\n| 2 | 79.668 MHz | Divider partial-remainder feedback |\n\nThe design uses 4,307 logic cells. The 79.668 MHz median gives **248.75 CoreMark iterations/s**, below the 90.75 MHz promotion threshold at this cycle count. The selected implementation had the highest median among the page-split implementations measured locally.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
