{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_21", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,170p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-002-r12s1.yaml\npython3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport json\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/run_summary.json')\na=json.loads(p.read_text())\nprint('summary fields:',list(a))\nfor k,v in a.items():\n    print(k,len(v) if isinstance(v,(dict,list)) else str(v)[:120])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261119-002-r12s1\ntitle: \"Share the registered address path with JALR\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion reaches 270.88 CoreMark iterations/s at 86.757 MHz\n  in 3,202,739 bracketed cycles. Its median Gowin placements trace\n  the worst path from registered dependency matches through EX\n  forwarding, the JALR carry chain and the full target comparison.\n  The round-11 carry-select trial moved the bottleneck to fetch and\n  lowered Fmax. A local retirement trace counted 13,735 JALRs in\n  the timed window, all with a zero immediate. The common JALR\n  can validate its successor by comparing the forwarded base\n  directly, freeing the address generator to resolve every JALR\n  in parallel with validation.\nhypothesis: |\n  Route JALR through the existing load/store address generator.\n  The live load-data input to this adder is enabled only for a\n  younger load. JALR therefore adds the full forwarded rs1 and\n  sign-extended immediate after any required load-use replay.\n  Capture the result in the existing EX/MEM address field. Use a\n  separate two-bit add for JALR alignment and link-write permission.\n  Clear bit zero when the registered address becomes a JALR target\n  for recovery, BTB training and RVFI. Register the ID-computed\n  target in the same address field for direct control transfers,\n  which leaves one address register driving the target and keeps\n  target selection ahead of the recovery cycle.\n\n  Capture the zero-immediate predicate in ID. For a zero-immediate\n  JALR in EX, validate the full fetch PC against the forwarded rs1\n  with bit zero cleared, subject to the existing parked-PC and\n  alignment rules. The comparison finishes independently of the\n  full add. For a nonzero immediate, conservatively take the\n  existing taken recovery path from the registered target on\n  every aligned advance. Fetch can still accept a ready corrected\n  word in that cycle. Train the BTB from the actual target and\n  keep the full-width checks for direct and fall-through paths.\n  Advance and train once through stalls. Preserve load/store\n  addresses and masks, alignment traps, redirects, FENCE.I and\n  in-order nret=1 retirement.\n\n  The predicted gain comes from removing both the serial add and\n  compare and the duplicate full-width JALR adder. At the\n  champion's cycle count the promotion threshold is 90.75 MHz.\n  With a 0.5 percent cycle increase it rises to 91.19 MHz.\n  A 93 MHz median at that cycle cost would give about 6.7 percent\n  higher fitness. Replay and the divider are nearly tied timing\n  risks. Check zero and nonzero immediates, JALR bit-zero clearing,\n  dependent loads, misaligned targets, stall and squash behavior\n  and the canonical CoreMark markers and CRCs.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Carry the zero-immediate predicate in ID/EX and define the EX/MEM address payload for both LSU addresses and resolved control targets.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Compute the zero-immediate predicate from the fetched encoding and keep it with the instruction through stalls and load replay.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Share the address generator and EX/MEM address register, validate zero-immediate JALR by comparing the forwarded base, and recover aligned nonzero-immediate JALR from the registered target.\"\nsummary fields: ['iterations', 'accepted', 'rejected', 'broken', 'broken_by_class', 'baseline_fitness', 'final_fitness', 'best_fitness', 'best_round', 'delta_pct', 'best_lut4', 'best_ff', 'best_fmax_mhz', 'best_iterations', 'best_cycles', 'best_ipc_coremark']\niterations 34\naccepted 11\nrejected 23\nbroken 0\nbroken_by_class 0\nbaseline_fitness 12.12\nfinal_fitness 270.88\nbest_fitness 270.88\nbest_round 31\ndelta_pct 2134.983498349835\nbest_lut4 4393\nbest_ff 994\nbest_fmax_mhz 86.757\nbest_iterations 10\nbest_cycles 3202739\nbest_ipc_coremark 3e-06\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
