nd the ALU into the timed data-memory logic. The ALU infers\\ncombinational division and remainder for both signed and unsigned inputs,\\nmaking them the leading suspect for the long ALU path. The baseline\\nVerilator retirement trace reproduced all 4,491,485 marker-bracketed\\ncycles with both bus stalls active and contained zero DIV or REM fa\n…[64412 of 67484 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:ecd91ad717573ea4]…\nplacement options 0, 1, and 2 measured 52.133, 53.281, and 53.281 MHz. Option 0 used 3,386 logic cells and 725 registers. At the 53.281 MHz median, CoreMark measured 127.12 iter/s, 11.45% above the 114.06 iter/s champion and above the 4.6% acceptance margin.\\n\\nThe measured median is 2.282 MHz below the scratch prototype. The longest setup path in all three placements runs from the register file to the EX/MEM ALU result register. The divider recurrence is close behind in both median placements.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-19T15:12:45.311482Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 3, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r3s0 (improvement, +11.45%): Replacing five serial restoring steps with two radix-4 steps using parallel divisor trials and one radix-2 step shortened the divider recurrence, and registering the unmasked JALR sum for load/store addresses bypassed the ALU result mux, together raising median Gowin Fmax from 47.807 to 53.281 MHz and shifting the critical path to the register-file-to-EX/MEM ALU result path.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
