{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'tail -5 cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'", "aggregated_output": "{\"id\": \"hyp-20261119-003-r6s2\", \"title\": \"Replay two instruction words at predicted taken targets\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The champion's BTB predicts the next address, but every correctly\\npredicted taken transfer still waits for the instruction bus to supply\\nthe first word at that address. CoreMark repeatedly returns to hot loop\\nentries and function targets. A small branch target instruction cache\\ncan replay the first two words from those destinations while the bus\\nfetches their continuation. This hides instruction-bus stalls even\\non cycles with no older data-bus hold.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The champion's BTB predicts the next address, but every correctly\\npredicted taken transfer still waits for the instruction bus to supply\\nthe first word at that address. CoreMark repeatedly returns to hot loop\\nentries and function targets. A small branch target instruction cache\\ncan replay the first two words from those destinations while the bus\\nfetches their continuation. This hides instruction-bus stalls even\\non cycles with no older data-bus hold.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Keep the 32-entry BTB and add a 16-entry direct-mapped branch target\\ninstruction cache indexed by PC[5:2] and tagged with PC[19:6]. Each\\nentry holds the first word at an aligned target below 1 MiB and its\\nsequential word at PC+4, with independent valid bits and unreset word\\narrays. Fill the first word from an accepted imem response at a\\nBTB-predicted taken destination or a resolved taken recovery address.\\nAn accepted buffered copy of an imem response also qualifies. Keep\\nthe recovery fill eligible through a hold and clear the second-word\\nvalid bit on a first-word replacement. On a first-word replay with\\nsequential continuation and no valid second word, learn the second\\nword from a ready bus response at PC+4 when ID accepts the first word.\\n\\nRecord when the BTB chooses a taken destination as the next logical\\nfetch PC. An exact cache hit on that PC can supply the first word to\\nID regardless of imemReady. Whenev\n…[38776 of 41848 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:6f3fd036fd624cd0]…\n, zero load-use bubbles on ready responses, and stalls.\\n- RVFI cosim passed selftest and all three random programs with both buses ready and with both stall streams enabled. CoreMark matched the canonical CRCs and completed 10 iterations in 3,337,377 marker-bracketed cycles.\\n- The measured Fmax and CoreMark cycle count yield 227.41 iterations/s, up 6.86% from the 212.81 iterations/s champion.\\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-channel ALTOPS checks on the final RTL, including register consistency. The run used an isolated scratch copy of the standard harness.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-19T18:52:59.168456Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 8, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r8s0 (regression, +6.86%): Using a registered EX/MEM index to share the low-byte mux before per-destination load gating shortened the RAM-to-shifter forwarding path and exposed a divider-limited 75.894 MHz Gowin median, with unchanged CoreMark cycles leaving throughput behind the round winner's stall-reducing fetch replay.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
