{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'tail -n 14 cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'", "aggregated_output": "{\"id\": \"hyp-20261119-001-r2s0\", \"title\": \"Squash decode through the ID/EX valid bit\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The registered-divider champion reaches 46.963 MHz and reports a 27.746 ns\\nsetup path from the ID/EX source register through forwarding, EX, IF and\\nhazard control back to an ID/EX control register. EX redirect currently\\nselects a replacement instruction in IF and clears the wide ID/EX payload.\\nThat puts late branch resolution on instruction decode, load-use matching\\nand a high-fanout payload clear. The same squash can be expressed by changing\\nonly the validity of the decode slot.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The registered-divider champion reaches 46.963 MHz and reports a 27.746 ns\\nsetup path from the ID/EX source register through forwarding, EX, IF and\\nhazard control back to an ID/EX control register. EX redirect currently\\nselects a replacement instruction in IF and clears the wide ID/EX payload.\\nThat puts late branch resolution on instruction decode, load-use matching\\nand a high-fanout payload clear. The same squash can be expressed by changing\\nonly the validity of the decode slot.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Feed the raw imem word to decode and carry fetch readiness separately. Hold\\nthe ID/EX payload and validity during data backpressure or a divider wait.\\nOn other cycles, capture the payload and set ID/EX validity to\\n`imemReady && !load_use && !redirect`. Reset clears validity. Keep the PC\\nredirect in EX with the existing priority over fetch holds. This removes the\\nredirect-to-NOP mux and the redirect-dependent payload clear at the current\\npipeline depth and redirect latency.\\n\\nRequire a valid EX instruction for redirects and divider launch. Gate the\\nload-use check with ID/EX validity and fetch readiness, keeping the current\\nsource-register match policy. On EX/MEM advance, qualify mem_read, mem_write\\nand the post-trap reg_write with `id_ex.valid && !div_wait`. The resulting\\ncontrols protect memory requests, hazard detection and forwarding from\\ninvalid slots. During \n…[134709 of 137781 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:876d24b524828b23]…\n on selftest and all three random programs with and without bus stalls. Stalled CoreMark matched the five canonical CRCs in **3,987,921 marker-bracketed cycles** for 10 iterations.\\n\\n| Gowin placement option | Fmax (MHz) |\\n| --- | ---: |\\n| 0 | 71.789 |\\n| 1 | 73.329 |\\n| 2 | 73.329 |\\n\\nOption 0 used 3,342 logic cells and 887 registers. The 73.329 MHz median gives **183.88 CoreMark iterations/s**, up **8.70%** from 169.16. The worst option-0 path runs from data RAM to the registered BTB counter. Options 1 and 2 are limited by the path from the registered taken decision to the IF next-PC register.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-19T17:39:50.766833Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 6, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-002-r6s1 (improvement, +8.70%): Registering address-derived byte-lane and sign-extension masks in EX lets MEM apply them directly to RAM data with each operand's one-hot bypass enable, keeping load-format decode off the forwarding path while preserving immediate load-use issue and CoreMark cycle count.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
