{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\na=[json.loads(x) for x in Path(\\\"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\\").read_text().splitlines()]\nfor x in a:\n if x['id'].endswith(('r6s0','r7s1','r8s1','r9s1','r11s0','r11s2')):\n  print(x['id'], x['expected_impact'],x['critical_path'])\n  print(x['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261119-001-r6s0 {'fitness_delta_pct': 7, 'confidence': 'medium'} {'from': 'dmem_1_dmem_1_0_0_s', 'to': 'cpu/u_ex/reg_q.alu_result_23_s0', 'slack_ns': -9.185, 'arrival_ns': 19.479, 'logic_cells': 14, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_mem', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']}\nRetiming the late output selection for shifts and BTB training\ntogether should raise the clock while preserving the existing\nissue schedule. Give SLL, SRL and SRA a dedicated combinational\nresult and capture it and its selector alongside the ordinary\nALU result in EX/MEM. In MEM, mux shift versus ordinary data\nfor the EX ALU bypass and add the shift lane to the one-hot\nMEM/WB result selection. Support register and immediate shift\nforms with the forwarded A operand and the low five bits of\nthe selected B operand. Hold both lanes and the selector\ntogether under dmem backpressure. Qualify forwarding with the\nexisting writer checks and dependency priority.\n\nUse the same edge to capture independent BTB training facts.\nRegister advance and legal source-PC qualification, target range,\nthe taken-alignment fault, the branch-and-hit predicate, and the\nsaturating increment and decrement counter candidates. Record the\noutcome used to select the candidate. Combine these after the\nedge with the registered aligned-taken bit and target already\ncaptured for fetch recovery. Derive target write from the source\nqualification, aligned taken result and target range. Derive\ncounter write from a target write or a qualified, hit conditional\nbranch with no taken-alignment fault. Jumps select the strongly\ntaken counter and a new taken branch selects weakly taken. Feed\nboth existing pending-update bypasses so a same-index lookup,\nheld ID/EX candidate or consecutive update sees the write in its\noriginal cycle. Only an advancing, live instruction creates a\ntraining record and reset suppresses writes.\n\nThe scratch prototype placed at 71.151, 72.012 and 72.012 MHz\nusing 3,460 Gowin logic cells and 847 registers. CoreMark passed\nthe canonical CRCs in 3,987,921 mar\n…[13125 of 16197 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:35390747784dbea9]…\nntries on a not-taken outcome. Train once per\nadvancing EX instruction. Pack validity with the tag and target and\nclear the entries over 32 clocks at reset. Give clearing priority\nover training writes. During the clear, predict sequentially and\ncontinue ordinary fetch and EX recovery.\n\nDrop BTB hit and counter fields from ID/EX and remove the metadata\nrepair path. The first ordinary lookup after a training write sees\nthe committed state. Keep full-width EX successor validation, the\nexisting recovery priority and same-cycle corrected bus acceptance,\nand the replay store's reset, FENCE.I and stall handling.\n\nA scratch implementation completed stalled CoreMark with all canonical\nCRCs in 3,202,739 bracketed cycles. Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2\nmeasured 80.455, 82.752 and 82.752 MHz, projecting 258.38 iter/s.\nThis is 4.62% below the champion. The median critical path moved to\nEX forwarding into the shifter, with the divider nearly tied. At\nthese cycles a qualifying result requires a median above 90.75 MHz.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
