{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_12", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg 'hyp-20261119-(002-r11s1|002-r2s1|003-r8s2|003-r9s2|002-r10s1|002-r12s1|003-r12s2)' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import json,sys; [(print(r[\\\"id\\\"],r[\\\"title\\\"],r[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"motivation\\\"],r[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"] if r[\\\"id\\\"] in {\\\"hyp-20261119-002-r11s1\\\",\\\"hyp-20261119-002-r10s1\\\"} else \\\"\\\")) for x in sys.stdin if (r:=json.loads(x)).get(\\\"hypothesis\\\")]'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261119-002-r2s1 Decouple fetch with one fall-through instruction slot The 46.963 MHz champion holds the fetch PC whenever a load-use hazard,\ndata-memory stall or divide blocks decode. A ready instruction response\nduring that hold is lost and the same address may face an imem stall\nwhen decode resumes. The champion also replaces the fetched word with\na NOP on EX redirect. Its reported 27.746 ns worst path runs from EX\nforwarding through that flush and back to the ID/EX JALR decode bit.\nAn elastic fetch boundary can save a ready response and squash by\nvalidity, letting decode see the selected instruction word directly.\n \nhyp-20261119-003-r8s2 Keep BTB-armed instruction replay active in a larger store The current 32-word BTB-armed replay window improved the timed CoreMark run\nfrom 3,987,921 to 3,337,377 cycles at a 71.024 MHz median. The backing store\nholds 128 instructions, so stream PCs 512 bytes apart can evict each\nother. The window also expires after 32 accepted words on long paths\nthrough repeatedly executed code. Expanding the replay store to 512\nwords alone saved 1.64% of cycles in scratch simulation. Retaining\neligibility over longer streams and fitting more of their instructions\nin the store saved 4.35%.\n \nhyp-20261119-003-r9s2 Pack validity into a 4K-word BTB-armed replay RAM The champion runs canonical stalled CoreMark in 3,192,109 cycles at a\nmedian 74.271 MHz. Its 1,024-word BTB-armed replay store aliases PCs\n4 KiB apart and uses a separately reset 1,024-bit validity map. A\nlarger store can retain more recurring predicted-path instructions.\nPacking validity with the replay word removes the indexed validity\nmux and shifts capacity growth from logic cells to block RAM.\n \nhyp-20261119-002-r10s1 Hold the replay read with the fetch PC Bench delivers 251.12 CoreMark iterations/s at 80.427 MHz over\n3,202,739 marker-bracketed cycles. A Gowin rerun traced a 17.728 ns\nsetup path from normal_pc_q[11] through replay validity, fetch acceptance\nand the next-PC mux to the replay word RAM. When a request holds, \n…[7036 of 10108 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:ad0c3dcfb9f73403]…\ntrial moved the bottleneck to fetch and\nlowered Fmax. A local retirement trace counted 13,735 JALRs in\nthe timed window, all with a zero immediate. The common JALR\ncan validate its successor by comparing the forwarded base\ndirectly, freeing the address generator to resolve every JALR\nin parallel with validation.\n \nhyp-20261119-003-r12s2 Validate JALR predictions with parallel carry checks The bench champion scores 270.88 CoreMark iterations per second at\n86.757 MHz over 3,202,739 timed cycles. The reproduced Gowin option 1\nbuild puts a path from the registered dependency match through JALR\ntarget addition into the candidate-PC equality register. An instrumented\nrun counted 58,391 timed corrections, including 1,982 JALR corrections.\nThe limited cycle savings favor shortening the predictor's validation\npath. Round 11's carry-select variant generated alternative byte sums.\nThe predicted address itself provides the carry that each bit must see,\nallowing each bit's carry consistency to be checked independently.\n \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
