{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(line)\n print(d['id'], d['category'], d.get('fitness'), d.get('cycles'), d.get('seeds'), d.get('critical_path'), 'DSP',d.get('dsp'))\n if d.get('round_id')==3 or d['category']=='micro_opt' and d.get('round_id')\"'!=0:\n  print(d.get('\"'implementation_notes',''))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "baseline-bench-fe54f00 micro_opt 12.12 4491485 [5.14, 5.442, 5.442] {'from': 'cpu/u_mem/reg_q.rd_3_s0', 'to': 'dmem_0_dmem_0_0_0_s', 'slack_ns': -189.542, 'arrival_ns': 199.837, 'logic_cells': 156, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_fwd', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']} DSP 6\nhyp-20261119-001-r1s0 micro_opt 93.78 4491485 [41.866, 42.123, 42.123] {'from': 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu/remainder_q_2_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu/remainder_q_11_s0', 'slack_ns': -18.886, 'arrival_ns': 29.18, 'logic_cells': 189, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu']} DSP 6\n# Shared six-round divider\n\nThe EX ALU now shares one unsigned restoring divider across DIV, DIVU, REM and REMU. It captures the forwarded operands, runs six rounds of six radix-2 steps, and transfers the result on the eighth EX cycle when MEM can advance. The divider registers the partial remainder and reuses a 36-bit shift register for the padded dividend and outgoing quotient bits. ALTOPS captures the existing stand-in result and uses the same handshake and latency.\n\nThe divider derives signed results from unsigned magnitudes. A zero divisor naturally leaves the dividend magnitude in the remainder and produces an all-ones quotient. Sign restoration preserves the original dividend for a zero-divisor remainder and skips quotient negation in that case. The 32-bit magnitude and result also preserve `INT_MIN / -1` and its zero remainder.\n\nFetch and decode hold while EX waits. When MEM advances during the divide, EX/MEM captures a bubble with inactive controls, letting older instructions retire. A divide captures its forwarded values for RVFI at issue and passes through EX/MEM once, where the usual bypass serves the next instruction. The core retains its single-channel RVFI interface.\n\nA guarded include brings the core-wide types into scope for the requested alphabetical Verilator glob. Fetch flush is driven independently of the load-use check to remove Verilator's apparent feedback path in VPI builds.\n\n## Local checks\n\n- Strict Verilator lint passed in hardware and ALTO\n…[11442 of 14514 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:3151d8a39ec6b8d3]…\nrun_all.sh` passed all 53 single-retire ALTOPS checks on the final RTL. The unchanged harness ran from a scratch copy under `$TMPDIR`. Zero retries were needed for a formal failure.\n- Full RVFI cosimulation passed for `selftest.elf` and `random1.elf` through `random3.elf` with and without bus stalls.\n- CoreMark passed the canonical CRCs (`e9f5`, `e714`, `1fd7`, `8e3a`, `fcaf`) and printed `Correct operation validated.` Both buses stalled. The start and stop markers bracketed 3,959,069 cycles, a 5.54% reduction from 4,191,341, with no out-of-bounds accesses reported.\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 measured 48.270, 49.095, and 49.095 MHz. The 49.095 MHz median exceeds the 47.235 MHz floor and yields 124.01 CoreMark iterations per second, up 8.72% from 114.06.\n- The divider limited placements 1 and 2. Placement 0 was limited by the path from the register file through EX to data RAM. The option 0 report shows 3,810 logic cells and 1,144 registers, up from the parent's 3,030 logic cells and 659 registers.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
