{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_61", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,120p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-001-r4s0.yaml\"", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261119-001-r4s0\ntitle: \"Split RV32M arithmetic across existing register boundaries\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The 127.12 iter/s champion reaches 53.281 MHz median Gowin Fmax. Its\n  worst setup path runs through multiply and the ALU result select into\n  EX/MEM. The divider recurrence is close behind in both median placements.\n  A trace of the champion under both bus stalls counted 93,960 MULs and\n  no high-half multiplies, divides or remainders in 4,191,341 bracketed\n  cycles. Moving product reduction across EX/MEM can keep one multiply\n  per cycle for independent instructions. Shorter divider updates let\n  the higher clock rate carry through to the full core.\nhypothesis: |\n  Generate four 16-bit-limb products in EX using independent DSP-sized\n  multipliers directly from the forwarded operands. Zero-extend both low\n  limbs and extend each high limb according to the opcode's signedness.\n  Capture the partial products and low/high select with EX/MEM. In MEM,\n  form a 48-bit sum of the two sign-extended cross products and the row\n  made by concatenating the high-high product with bits 31:16 of the\n  low-low product. Use a carry-save reduction and one carry-propagate\n  sum. Concatenate its low 16 bits with bits 15:0 of the low-low product\n  for MUL, or take its high 32 bits for the high-half ops.\n\n  Register the final writeback data in MEM/WB after selecting among load\n  data, the reduced product and the ordinary EX result. This removes the\n  WB data mux from the forwarding and regfile write paths. Treat multiply\n  as a late producer in the existing load-use interlock and keep its\n  unfinished value out of the EX/MEM bypass. Match both raw source fields\n  as the core does for loads so RVFI sees the correct values for those\n  fields. Exclude x0. Independent instructions can still issue every\n  cycle. An adjacent matching instruction gets one bubble and forwards\n  from MEM/WB. Hold the partial products with EX/MEM under backpressure\n  and preserve MEM/WB's existing retained bypass data on a stall. Us\n…[2217 of 5289 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:710e7d71bbf015a3]…\nve logic and select the final writeback data before the MEM/WB register, preserving its data-hold behavior.\"\n  - file: rtl/wb_stage.sv\n    description: \"Use the registered writeback data for RF writes and forwarding while retaining write and retirement qualification.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Treat valid multiply and load instructions as late producers for hazard detection and exclude unfinished products from the EX/MEM bypass.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Generalize the existing nonzero-destination load-use interlock to late results from either loads or multiplies.\"\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: \"Exercise the staged multiply datapath with the existing exact-value cases and add narrow-divider boundary cases for divisor magnitudes 255 and 256.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Check independent multiply issue, dependent multiply bypass through WB, load-to-multiply and multiply-to-store dependencies, and RVFI values under reset and backpressure.\"\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_62","type":"agent_message","text":"Wrote and schema-validated the `micro_opt` hypothesis for `cores/bench`. [hyp-20261119-001-r4s0.yaml](/srv/hwebench/clones/gpt-6_2-sol_xhigh-v2-rep1/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20261119-001-r4s0-hypgen/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-001-r4s0.yaml)\n\nThe scratch prototype split multiply across EX and MEM and shortened the divider’s per-cycle logic. It reached **139.82 CoreMark iter/s**, **9.99% above** the champion, at **59.112 MHz** median Gowin Fmax. It passed the canonical CoreMark CRC checks, all 53 single-retire ALTOPS checks, and RV32M arithmetic cosimulation."}}
