{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"category\\\": \\\"micro_opt\\\"|\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20261119-002-r4s1\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json,sys; fields=\\\"id title fitness fmax_mhz cycles critical_path logic_levels perturbations implementation_notes\\\".split(); [print(json.dumps({k:d.get(k) for k in fields},indent=2)) for d in map(json.loads,sys.stdin)]'\"", "aggregated_output": "{\n  \"id\": \"baseline-bench-fe54f00\",\n  \"title\": \"Baseline retest for cores/bench\",\n  \"fitness\": 12.12,\n  \"fmax_mhz\": 5.442,\n  \"cycles\": 4491485,\n  \"critical_path\": {\n    \"from\": \"cpu/u_mem/reg_q.rd_3_s0\",\n    \"to\": \"dmem_0_dmem_0_0_0_s\",\n    \"slack_ns\": -189.542,\n    \"arrival_ns\": 199.837,\n    \"logic_cells\": 156,\n    \"modules\": [\n      \"clock_ibuf\",\n      \"cpu/u_fwd\",\n      \"cpu/u_ex\",\n      \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu\",\n      \"cpu/u_ex\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"logic_levels\": 150,\n  \"perturbations\": [\n    [\n      0,\n      5.14,\n      150\n    ],\n    [\n      1,\n      5.442,\n      149\n    ],\n    [\n      2,\n      5.442,\n      149\n    ]\n  ],\n  \"implementation_notes\": \"\"\n}\n{\n  \"id\": \"hyp-20261119-001-r1s0\",\n  \"title\": \"Use a shared six-round divider in execute\",\n  \"fitness\": 93.78,\n  \"fmax_mhz\": 42.123,\n  \"cycles\": 4491485,\n  \"critical_path\": {\n    \"from\": \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu/remainder_q_2_s0\",\n    \"to\": \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu/remainder_q_11_s0\",\n    \"slack_ns\": -18.886,\n    \"arrival_ns\": 29.18,\n    \"logic_cells\": 189,\n    \"modules\": [\n      \"clock_ibuf\",\n      \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"logic_levels\": 36,\n  \"perturbations\": [\n    [\n      0,\n      41.866,\n      36\n    ],\n    [\n      1,\n      42.123,\n      36\n    ],\n    [\n      2,\n      42.123,\n      36\n    ]\n  ],\n  \"implementation_notes\": \"# Shared six-round divider\\n\\nThe EX ALU now shares one unsigned restoring divider across DIV, DIVU, REM and REMU. It captures the forwarded operands, runs six rounds of six radix-2 steps, and transfers the result on the eighth EX cycle when MEM can advance. The divider registers the partial remainder and reuses a 36-bit shift register for the padded dividend and outgoing quotient bits. ALTOPS captures the existing stand-in result and uses the same handshake and latency.\\n\\nThe divider derives signed results from unsigned magnitudes. A zero divisor naturally leaves the dividend magnitude in the remainder and produces an all-ones quotient. Sign restoration preserves the original dividend for a zero-divisor remainder and skips quotient negation in that case. The 32\n…[12462 of 15534 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:f9233a27734b50b1]…\nilures. The real multiply and divide arithmetic was exercised by cocotb and co-simulation.\\n- All five cocotb suites passed. The pipeline tests cover consecutive load-to-ALU and load-to-multiply retirement, multiply chaining, byte and halfword formatting, load-to-divide latency, trap forwarding priority, and stalled load forwarding to stores and branches.\\n- Full-trace co-simulation passed on selftest and all three random programs with no stalls and with both stall streams enabled.\\n- CoreMark validated all canonical CRCs in 3,987,921 marker-bracketed cycles for 10 iterations with both stall streams enabled.\\n\\n| Gowin placement option | Fmax (MHz) |\\n| --- | ---: |\\n| 0 | 62.201 |\\n| 1 | 61.972 |\\n| 2 | 61.972 |\\n| Median | 61.972 |\\n\\nOption 0 used 3,211 logic cells, 719 registers, and two DSP resources. The worst option 0 path runs from data-memory readback to fetch-PC recovery. The median clock and bracketed cycles yield 155.40 CoreMark iterations per second, 22.25% above the 127.12 iter/s champion.\\n\"\n}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
