{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg 'hyp-20261119-00[123]-r7s[012]|hyp-20261119-00[12]-r6s[01]' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import json,sys\nfor l in sys.stdin:\n d=json.loads(l)\n print(d[\\\"id\\\"], d[\\\"fmax_mhz\\\"],d[\\\"cycles\\\"],d[\\\"fitness\\\"], d[\\\"seeds\\\"], d[\\\"critical_path\\\"],sep=\\\"\\\\n\\\")\n print(d[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"][:5400])\n'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261119-001-r6s0\n71.49\n3987921\n179.27\n[70.499, 71.49, 71.49]\n{'from': 'dmem_1_dmem_1_0_0_s', 'to': 'cpu/u_ex/reg_q.alu_result_23_s0', 'slack_ns': -9.185, 'arrival_ns': 19.479, 'logic_cells': 14, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_mem', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']}\n# EX/MEM result and BTB retiming\n\n- EX/MEM captures the shift result and selector alongside the ordinary ALU result and holds them together through dmem backpressure. EX forwarding selects the registered shift or ordinary result under the existing writer and dependency checks. Load and multiply keep their direct bypass lanes. MEM/WB selects among the four result lanes with one-hot masks.\n- The standalone ALU still returns shifts on its ordinary output. SRL and SRA share a right shifter with a selectable fill bit. The shifter uses the forwarded operand and the low five bits of the selected register or immediate operand.\n- EX registers source eligibility, target range, the taken-alignment fault, branch hit, jump status, and both saturated counter candidates. IF and ID receive the pending update in the recovery cycle using the registered recovery target. The registered aligned-taken bit also selects the counter candidate because a taken alignment fault suppresses training. Source eligibility requires a live, advancing, legal instruction with an in-range, word-aligned PC. Reset clears eligibility.\n- The directed pipeline test runs with a ready load and with four stalled load cycles. It checks younger-load priority over an older write to the same register, SRA with a register amount of 63, all three immediate shifts, consecutive RVFI retirements, and the accepted dependent store.\n\n## Local checks\n\n- Verilator lint passed in hardware and ALTOPS modes. The single-retire ALTOPS formal gate passed all 53 checks with zero failures and no corrective retries.\n- All five cocotb suites passed, including same-PC counter bypass, same-index tag replacement, branch and JALR recovery, and both load-to-shift cases. RVFI cosimulation passed for selftest and\n…[9044 of 12116 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:9394a1fffcf1aa94]…\nrk passed with both stall streams and no out-of-bounds accesses. The UART reported `Correct operation validated.` and all five canonical CRCs (`e9f5`, `e714`, `1fd7`, `8e3a`, `fcaf`).\n\n## Performance\n\n`make fpga TARGET=bench` measured 3,337,377 marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles for 10 iterations. The starting RTL took 3,987,921 cycles with the same ELF and stall streams, a reduction of 16.31%. The implementation finished 29,715 cycles ahead of the scratch prototype.\n\n| Gowin placement option | Fmax (MHz) |\n| --- | ---: |\n| 0 | 70.632 |\n| 1 | 71.024 |\n| 2 | 71.024 |\n| Median | 71.024 |\n\nOption 0 used 3,698 logic cells, 1,041 registers, and six BSRAMs. Its worst setup path ran from data RAM to the EX shift-result register. The median clears the 64.77 MHz floor and yields 212.81 iterations/s, 15.74% above 183.88. It is 1.934 MHz and 3.87 iterations/s below the scratch prototype's median and projected score. Two equivalent fetch-PC selector rewrites measured lower median Fmax, so the faster selector is retained.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
