{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_136","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,120p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-001-r8s0.yaml\npython3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport os\nroot=Path(os.environ[\\\"TMPDIR\\\"])/\\\"shift-study\\\"\nfor entry,id in ((\\\"binary\\\",\\\"binary\\\"),(\\\"fcleanidx\\\",\\\"cleanidx\\\")):\n q=root/entry/f\\\"formal/riscv-formal/cores/bench-{id}/checks\\\"\n done=[(s.stem,(s.with_suffix(\\\"\\\")/\\\"status\\\").read_text().split()[0]) for s in q.glob(\\\"*.sby\\\") if (s.with_suffix(\\\"\\\")/\\\"status\\\").is_file()]\n print(entry,len(done),[x for x in done if x[1]\"'!=\"PASS\"])\n for place in (0,1,2):\n  p=root/entry/f\"gowin_p{place}/gw.log\"\n  print(place, p.read_text().splitlines()[-2:])\nPY\ndate -u\n'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261119-001-r8s0\ntitle: \"Select the forwarded load byte with a registered index\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The 212.81 iter/s bench champion runs ten CoreMark iterations in\n  3,337,377 marker-bracketed cycles at a 71.024 MHz Gowin median. Its\n  slowest reported placement puts data RAM readback through the load\n  formatter and EX operand network on the path to the shift result.\n  The low byte of each formatted response currently uses four masked\n  data lanes, with separate load qualification for both forwarded\n  operands and writeback. A single indexed byte selection can serve\n  all three consumers and reduce the late RAM data routing.\nhypothesis: |\n  Replace the four-bit low-byte mask with a two-bit index registered\n  with the EX/MEM load-format controls. A word load selects byte 0,\n  a halfword load selects byte 0 or 2 using address bit 1, and a byte\n  load selects address bits [1:0]. In the MEM formatter, select\n  data[{byte_index, 3'b0} +: 8] from the raw memory response. Gate\n  the selected byte with each destination's existing load-select bit\n  after the selection, so all three destinations can share the mux.\n  Continue using the existing registered masks for the upper bytes\n  and sign bits. Preserve misaligned-access suppression, forwarding\n  priority, and EX/MEM holds so dependent instructions still issue\n  with zero load-use bubbles on a ready response.\n\n  A scratch prototype measured 73.324, 74.310, and 74.310 MHz in the\n  three Gowin placements. With both stall streams enabled it matched\n  the canonical CoreMark CRCs in 3,337,377 bracketed cycles, yielding\n  222.66 iter/s from the timing median, a 4.63% gain. All five existing\n  cocotb suites passed, including signed and unsigned loads across\n  every legal byte and halfword lane. The gain is close to the 4.6%\n  acceptance margin. Reproduce all three placements, RVFI cosim and\n  formal checks on the implementation.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Replace low_bytes with a two-bit byte_index in the EX/MEM load-format bundle.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Derive the low-byte index from the decoded load width and effective address bits, then capture it on the existing EX/MEM write edge.\"\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \"Use an indexed eight-bit selection for result bits 7:0 and apply each destination's load enable after that selection. Retain the existing upper-byte formatting and forwarding qualification.\"\nbinary 52 []\n0 ['Thu Nov 19 19:30:53 2026', '']\n1 ['Thu Nov 19 19:26:31 2026', '']\n2 ['Thu Nov 19 19:30:28 2026', '']\nfcleanidx 39 []\n0 ['Running routing......', '[60%] Routing Phase 0 completed']\n1 ['Thu Nov 19 19:32:11 2026', '']\n2 ['[70%] Routing Phase 1 completed', '[80%] Routing Phase 2 completed']\nThu Nov 19 18:34:52 UTC 2026\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
