", \"hypothesis\": \"Partition EX into a register-backed execution path and two live load\\npaths. Feed arithmetic, shifts, multiply, divider, ordered branches,\\nJALR and store data from register-backed forwarding. Give a dependent\\nyounger load's address adder a qualified raw-word bypass from a ready\\nLW, and give BEQ/BNE the full formatted load bypass into four byte\\nequality tests.\\nCapture the byte predicates at EX/MEM and resolve direction from them\\nin the e\n…[8241 of 11313 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:9ea833f4be7c1c9c]…\nRTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl bash formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 ALTOPS checks with the single-channel wrapper.\n- `NRET=1 make fpga TARGET=bench` measured 81.130, 80.427, and 80.427 MHz across the three Gowin placements. CoreMark completed ten iterations in 3,202,739 marker-bracketed cycles, yielding 251.12 iterations/s at the 80.427 MHz median.\n\nThe replay address path limits both placements at the median. The divider recurrence, ordered comparison, and register-backed shift paths are close to that limit. Compared with the starting core, the timed workload uses 10,630 more cycles.\n\ntimestamp : 2026-11-19T19:51:43.195915Z\nslot : 1\nround_id : 9\nlesson : - 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-002-r9s1 (improvement, +7.93%): Ready-LW address and formatted BEQ/BNE bypasses kept CoreMark's dominant load-use pairs bubble-free, while independent redirect acceptance and precomputing the ordinary fetch successor for each BTB training outcome let registered branch resolution feed synchronous replay RAM through a final address select.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
