{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"structural\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import json,sys\nfor l in sys.stdin:\n a=json.loads(l); print(a[\\\"id\\\"], a[\\\"title\\\"], a[\\\"fitness\\\"], a[\\\"cycles\\\"]); print(str(a[\\\"hypothesis\\\"])[:3100]); print(a[\\\"critical_path\\\"]); print()'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261119-002-r1s1 Share a registered execute-stage divider for DIV and REM 104.56 4491485\n{'motivation': 'The bench retest reaches 5.442 MHz and reports a 199.837 ns, 156-cell path through EX forwarding and the ALU to the data-memory path. The ALU feeds its common result mux with four combinational division and remainder expressions. The depth of an unrolled divider makes it the leading suspect for the low clock rate, and the mux exposes its delay even on cycles issuing ordinary instructions. A baseline Verilator retirement trace with both bus stalls enabled and all CoreMark CRCs validated counted zero DIV/REM instructions and 93,960 MUL instructions in the 4,491,485-cycle timed interval.\\n', 'hypothesis': 'Replace the four combinational DIV, DIVU, REM and REMU implementations with one blocking, registered divider in EX. On launch, capture operand magnitudes and sign controls, then compute 32 quotient bits over seven registered restoring-division updates, with two bit steps in the first update and five in each of the next six. Each step shifts in one dividend bit, uses a 33-bit trial subtract to choose the next partial remainder, and appends the corresponding quotient bit. Select the quotient or remainder after the last update, apply the appropriate sign for signed operations, and transfer it through the usual EX/MEM boundary. Handle zero divisors and INT_MIN divided by -1 with their exact RV32M results on the same schedule. Keep multiply and RV32I on the single-cycle ALU path so matrix multiplication, address generation and ordinary dependencies incur no new EX latency.\\n\\nLaunch once from a valid divide/remainder in ID/EX when the older memory stage can advance. Hold IF and ID/EX during the divide and insert EX/MEM bubbles after the older instruction advances, so older work drains exactly once. Capture the original forwarded operands at launch for RVFI and retain the result until EX/MEM accepts it. The completed instruction forwards and retires through the existing in-order path. Keep the same launch, wait \n…[34161 of 37233 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:1a0eae2758d33cc2]…\nand BTB training on the EX advance boundary.\\nCarry the resolved successor, trap status and memory metadata with the\\nsame completion slot. Suppress wrong-path allocation, preserve divider\\nlaunch and consume, and send MMIO stores only when they are the oldest\\ncompletion. Drain older requests before FENCE or FENCE.I advances and\\npreserve the existing FENCE.I refetch and invalidation. A request for a\\nyounger operation cannot pass an older memory request or an ordering\\nbarrier. Only an accepted bus request can complete a memory packet.\\nReset clears both queues.\\n\\nA scratch model replayed the 2,922,015 timed retirements with one EX\\nissue, one in-order retirement and one data-port acceptance per cycle,\\napproximately 22% data stalls and true load dependency interlocks. It\\ncounted 3,115,746 cy\n{'from': 'cpu/u_mem/ingress_writer_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_mem/writer_q_0_s1', 'slack_ns': -9.639, 'arrival_ns': 19.933, 'logic_cells': 14, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_fwd_rs1', 'cpu/u_id', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_mem']}\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
