{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d['id'].endswith(('r11s0','r11s2','r12s0','r12s2','r10s0','r10s1')):\n  print(d['id'], d['title'], d['seeds'], d['critical_path']); print(d['implementation_notes'][:6500]); print()\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261119-001-r10s0 Select fetch successors with one-hot enables [78.134, 83.372, 83.372] {'from': 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu/divisor_q_0_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu/partial_q_35_s1', 'slack_ns': -7.798, 'arrival_ns': 18.093, 'logic_cells': 66, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu']}\n# Select fetch successors with one-hot enables\n\n`cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv` now looks up the requested slot in both the replay RAM bitmap and the last written bitmap. The same-bank check selects between the two validity bits. The write side forwards the full bitmap so consecutive fills in a bank accumulate slot bits.\n\nSix enables select the held PC, PC+4, the table target, the pending write target, or either corrected successor in a single masked OR. Both prediction lanes share the resolved taken predicate and use the pending write only when taken training replaces the indexed target. Each correction uses its existing acceptance and last-word check to choose between the corrected address and its increment. The selected successor drives the PC register and both synchronous replay reads.\n\nThe RTL changes are confined to the fetch stage. Lint, all 61 directed pipeline cases, selftest co-simulation with and without memory stalls, and CoreMark CRC validation passed. A combinational SAT check against the previous RTL proved equivalent replay hits, ordinary acceptance, and successor addresses, and verified the six enables are one-hot outside reset.\n\nStalled CoreMark completed in 3,202,739 marker-bracketed cycles. Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 measured 78.134, 83.372, and 83.372 MHz. The 83.372 MHz median gives 260.32 iterations/s, up 3.66% from the 80.427 MHz champion at the same cycle count. Placement option 0 used 4,299 logic cells, up from 4,267, and 994 registers. The divider recurrence limits timing, and the median falls short of the 84.1266 MHz promotion threshold.\n\nLocal formal passed all 53 checks on the first run of the final RTL with the bench single-retirement configuration.\n\n\nhyp-20261119-002-r10s1 Hold the replay read with \n…[10234 of 13306 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:af126bd703fb2fa4]…\nbuses.\n- Stalled CoreMark validated all five CRCs without an out-of-bounds access. The start and stop markers were at cycles 12,468 and 3,215,207, giving 3,202,739 timed cycles.\n- The single-retirement `formal/run_all.sh` gate passed all 53 checks on the final RTL, including the depth-20 register-consistency check. It used `wrapper_si.sv` and `checks_si.cfg` in a scratch copy. No formal fixes were needed.\n\n## Gowin timing\n\n| RTL | Option 0 | Option 1 | Option 2 | Median Fmax | Logic cells |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Champion rerun | 76.343 MHz | 86.757 MHz | 86.757 MHz | 86.757 MHz | 4,393 |\n| Parallel carry checks | 76.896 MHz | 79.924 MHz | 79.924 MHz | 79.924 MHz | 4,342 |\n\nThe divider's partial-remainder feedback limits options 1 and 2. The fetch replay path limits option 0. At the measured cycle count the median gives 249.55 CoreMark iterations/s, 7.88% below the champion. The median also falls short of the supplied scratch result of 82.978 MHz and the 90.75 MHz promotion threshold.\n\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
