{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_5", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -in 'structural|redirect|critical path' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json,sys; [(lambda d: print(str(d)[:14000]))(json.loads(l.partition(\\\":\\\")[2])) for l in sys.stdin]'\"", "aggregated_output": "{'id': 'hyp-20261119-001-r1s0', 'title': 'Use a shared six-round divider in execute', 'category': 'micro_opt', 'motivation': 'The 5.442 MHz baseline has a 199.837 ns, 156-cell path through forwarding,\\nexecute and the ALU into the timed data-memory logic. The ALU infers\\ncombinational division and remainder for both signed and unsigned inputs,\\nmaking them the leading suspect for the long ALU path. The baseline\\nVerilator retirement trace reproduced all 4,491,485 marker-bracketed\\ncycles with both bus stalls active and contained zero DIV or REM family\\ninstructions inside that window. Both DIVU retirements were outside it.\\nSharing and registering division can cut ALU delay and area while adding\\nexecution cycles only to those four operations.\\n', 'hypothesis': {'motivation': 'The 5.442 MHz baseline has a 199.837 ns, 156-cell path through forwarding,\\nexecute and the ALU into the timed data-memory logic. The ALU infers\\ncombinational division and remainder for both signed and unsigned inputs,\\nmaking them the leading suspect for the long ALU path. The baseline\\nVerilator retirement trace reproduced all 4,491,485 marker-bracketed\\ncycles with both bus stalls active and contained zero DIV or REM family\\ninstructions inside that window. Both DIVU retirements were outside it.\\nSharing and registering division can cut ALU delay and area while adding\\nexecution cycles only to those four operations.\\n', 'hypothesis': \"In the existing EX stage, capture the forwarded operands and run one unsigned\\nrestoring divider for six cycles of six radix-2 steps each. Prepend four\\nzero bits to the 32-bit dividend magnitude so every round can reuse the\\nsame datapath. Each step shifts a dividend bit into the partial remainder,\\nuses a widened subtract and its borrow to select the next remainder, and\\nemits one quotient bit. Register quotient and remainder state each round.\\nFor signed operations, form unsigned 32-bit magnitudes and restore the\\nselected result's sign at completion. Use the XOR of operand signs for a\\nquotient and the\n…[25756 of 28828 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b9d24ae41b90dbfb]…\nrence with and without bus stalls. CoreMark passed all UART and CRC checks under both stalls and ran 4,215,937 bracketed cycles, down 6.13% from the supplied baseline.\\n- Gowin measured 5.179, 5.521, and 5.521 MHz for placement options 0, 1, and 2. The 5.521 MHz median gives 13.096 CoreMark iterations per second, up 8.05% from the supplied 12.12 baseline.\\n\\n## Timing and area\\n\\nThe BTB adds a tag check and target mux to the fetch PC path. The worst reported paths still traverse the EX ALU, with placement option 0 reporting 12,911 logic cells, 479 registers, and 17 LUT-RAM cells for the timed design.\\n', 'timestamp': '2026-11-19T14:33:35.953081Z', 'slot': 2, 'round_id': 1, 'lesson': \"- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r1s2 (regression, +8.09%): With combinational DIV/REM in EX still limiting the clock to 5.5 MHz, the 16-entry tagged BTB's 6.1% CoreMark cycle savings from reusing taken branch and jump targets under bus stalls were too small to close the throughput gap to the 47.0 MHz registered-divider variant.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
