{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg 'hyp-20261119-002-r1s1' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import sys,json; d=json.loads(next(sys.stdin)); print(d[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"]); print(d[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"])'\"","aggregated_output":"Replace the four combinational DIV, DIVU, REM and REMU implementations with one blocking, registered divider in EX. On launch, capture operand magnitudes and sign controls, then compute 32 quotient bits over seven registered restoring-division updates, with two bit steps in the first update and five in each of the next six. Each step shifts in one dividend bit, uses a 33-bit trial subtract to choose the next partial remainder, and appends the corresponding quotient bit. Select the quotient or remainder after the last update, apply the appropriate sign for signed operations, and transfer it through the usual EX/MEM boundary. Handle zero divisors and INT_MIN divided by -1 with their exact RV32M results on the same schedule. Keep multiply and RV32I on the single-cycle ALU path so matrix multiplication, address generation and ordinary dependencies incur no new EX latency.\n\nLaunch once from a valid divide/remainder in ID/EX when the older memory stage can advance. Hold IF and ID/EX during the divide and insert EX/MEM bubbles after the older instruction advances, so older work drains exactly once. Capture the original forwarded operands at launch for RVFI and retain the result until EX/MEM accepts it. The completed instruction forwards and retires through the existing in-order path. Keep the same launch, wait and completion schedule in ALTOPS mode with the existing substitute results. With both buses ready, eight added EX cycles plus one possible load-use or redirect bubble give at most ten cycles between consecutive retirements, within the unchanged liveness check window.\n\nThe experiment predicts a shorter Gowin critical path and fewer LUTs. The baseline trace implies zero direct divider waits inside the timed interval. Its two DIVU instructions outside that interval can shift the bus-stall sequence relative to the start marker, so measure the cycle count again. At the baseline cycle count, about 5.70 MHz median Gowin Fmax clears the acceptance threshold and 8.17 MHz gives approximately 50 percent higher fitness. Compare the new worst path delay and cell count against the retest and score the measured Fmax and validated CoreMark cycle count together.\n\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
