{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_5", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"structural\\\"|hyp-20261119-001-r3s0' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json,sys; [print(json.dumps(json.loads(line),indent=2)) for line in sys.stdin]'\"", "aggregated_output": "{\n  \"id\": \"hyp-20261119-002-r1s1\",\n  \"title\": \"Share a registered execute-stage divider for DIV and REM\",\n  \"category\": \"structural\",\n  \"motivation\": \"The bench retest reaches 5.442 MHz and reports a 199.837 ns, 156-cell path through EX forwarding and the ALU to the data-memory path. The ALU feeds its common result mux with four combinational division and remainder expressions. The depth of an unrolled divider makes it the leading suspect for the low clock rate, and the mux exposes its delay even on cycles issuing ordinary instructions. A baseline Verilator retirement trace with both bus stalls enabled and all CoreMark CRCs validated counted zero DIV/REM instructions and 93,960 MUL instructions in the 4,491,485-cycle timed interval.\\n\",\n  \"hypothesis\": {\n    \"motivation\": \"The bench retest reaches 5.442 MHz and reports a 199.837 ns, 156-cell path through EX forwarding and the ALU to the data-memory path. The ALU feeds its common result mux with four combinational division and remainder expressions. The depth of an unrolled divider makes it the leading suspect for the low clock rate, and the mux exposes its delay even on cycles issuing ordinary instructions. A baseline Verilator retirement trace with both bus stalls enabled and all CoreMark CRCs validated counted zero DIV/REM instructions and 93,960 MUL instructions in the 4,491,485-cycle timed interval.\\n\",\n    \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the four combinational DIV, DIVU, REM and REMU implementations with one blocking, registered divider in EX. On launch, capture operand magnitudes and sign controls, then compute 32 quotient bits over seven registered restoring-division updates, with two bit steps in the first update and five in each of the next six. Each step shifts in one dividend bit, uses a 33-bit trial subtract to choose the next partial remainder, and appends the corresponding quotient bit. Select the quotient or remainder after the last update, apply the appropriate sign for signed operations, and transfer it through the usual EX/MEM boundary. Handle zero divis\n…[33449 of 36521 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:e454948ca03cd625]…\n     47.252,\n      32\n    ],\n    [\n      1,\n      48.871,\n      32\n    ],\n    [\n      2,\n      48.871,\n      32\n    ]\n  ],\n  \"lutram\": 26,\n  \"bsram\": 6,\n  \"dsp\": 6,\n  \"critical_path\": {\n    \"from\": \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu/partial_q_32_s0\",\n    \"to\": \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu/partial_q_33_s0\",\n    \"slack_ns\": -16.163,\n    \"arrival_ns\": 26.457,\n    \"logic_cells\": 162,\n    \"modules\": [\n      \"clock_ibuf\",\n      \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"logic_levels\": 32,\n  \"formal_passed\": true,\n  \"cosim_passed\": true,\n  \"error\": null,\n  \"implementation_notes\": \"\",\n  \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-19T15:14:58.171539Z\",\n  \"slot\": 1,\n  \"round_id\": 3,\n  \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-002-r3s1 (regression, +7.44%): MEM-to-EX load forwarding removed load-use bubbles and cut CoreMark cycles by 4.85%, while registering the full JALR adder sum for load/store addresses kept the readback path out of the ALU result mux and restored divider-limited timing, leaving 122.55 iter/s behind the round winner's 127.12 iter/s from a shorter divider recurrence.\"\n}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
