{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_38", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor l in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n    d=json.loads(l)\n    if any(d['id'].endswith(x) for x in ('003-r3s2','003-r4s2','002-r2s1')):\n        print(d['id'], d['cycles'], d['fmax_mhz'])\n        print(d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\n        print(d['implementation_notes'][:3600])\nPY\ndate -u +%T\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261119-002-r2s1 4353657 48.337\nAdd one fall-through slot holding the instruction, its full PC and a\nvalid bit. Select the queued word and PC when occupied, otherwise offer\nthe live imem response directly to decode. Decode can issue a queued\nword even if imem is unready. Capture a ready response if the slot is\nempty and issue is blocked, and allow a full slot to issue its old word\nand capture a replacement on the same edge. Advance the fetch PC by\nfour only when a response bypasses into decode or is stored in the slot.\nA full slot that cannot issue keeps both its payload and the fetch PC.\n\nDerive issue permission from EX availability and the load-use check on\nthe selected head, independently of redirect. A valid advancing EX\nbranch or jump redirects the fetch PC directly at the edge, overriding\nsequential PC advance and imem backpressure. Clear the slot's valid bit\nand the younger ID/EX valid bit on that edge. Capture payload independently\nof squash, and clear the producer and memory controls when an invalid\nID/EX entry reaches EX/MEM. This keeps the EX decision out of the\ninstruction mux and decoder. The redirect target is on imem in the next\ncycle. An older blocked memory access defers EX advance and redirect\ntogether. Preserve target alignment checks, JALR bit-zero clearing and\nsingle-retire RVFI. Keep the older MEM/WB result available for forwarding\nto a held instruction after the result retires.\n\nA scratch prototype validated the canonical CoreMark CRCs in 4,353,657\nmarker-bracketed cycles with both bus stalls, down 3.07 percent from\n4,491,485. Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 measured 49.519, 49.118\nand 49.118 MHz. The median gives 112.82 iterations per second, 7.90\npercent above 104.56. The worst paths move to the register-file/EX\nmemory path and the existing divider. With the measured cycle count,\nacceptance requires median Fmax above 47.616 MHz. The prototype passed\nall 53 single-retire ALTOPS checks, the cocotb suites and full RVFI cosim\non selftest and the three random programs with and without \n…[8457 of 11529 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:510825ec5a0fa58d]…\nison. Train once per legal advancing control transfer and\nreport the resolved next PC to RVFI.\n\nA scratch prototype on the current arithmetic datapath validated the\ncanonical CoreMark CRCs under both bus stalls and ran 3,959,069 bracketed\ncycles, down 5.54% from 4,191,341. Gowin placement options measured\n50.075, 54.089 and 54.089 MHz. The 54.089 MHz median gives 136.62 iter/s,\nup 7.47% from 127.12. At that cycle count the acceptance floor is about\n52.64 MHz. The slowest placement is register-file to ALU limited and the\ntwo median placements report the divider recurrence. The scratch RTL\npassed all 53 single-retire ALTOPS formal checks, Verilator lint, all five\ncocotb suites and RVFI cosim on selftest and three random programs with\nand without stalls. Directed runs passed full-queue refill, boundary\nrecovery, FENCE.I refetch and suppression of a buffered wrong-path store\nwhen dmem backpressure releases while imem is unready. Recheck the cycle\ncount and three placement results after implementation.\n\n\n16:59:10\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
