{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_3", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n '\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"structural\\\"|DIV/REM|reduction' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | cut -c 1-540\"", "aggregated_output": "2:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261119-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Use a shared six-round divider in execute\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The 5.442 MHz baseline has a 199.837 ns, 156-cell path through forwarding,\\nexecute and the ALU into the timed data-memory logic. The ALU infers\\ncombinational division and remainder for both signed and unsigned inputs,\\nmaking them the leading suspect for the long ALU path. The baseline\\nVerilator retirement trace reproduced all 4,491,485 marker-bracketed\\ncycles with both bus stalls active and contained zer\n3:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261119-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Share a registered execute-stage divider for DIV and REM\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The bench retest reaches 5.442 MHz and reports a 199.837 ns, 156-cell path through EX forwarding and the ALU to the data-memory path. The ALU feeds its common result mux with four combinational division and remainder expressions. The depth of an unrolled divider makes it the leading suspect for the low clock rate, and the mux exposes its delay even on cycles issuing ordinary instructions. A ba\n4:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261119-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Predict branches and jumps with a 16-entry tagged BTB\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"Fetch and decode share a cycle in this core. Every taken branch or jump\\ncurrently discards one fetch slot while EX supplies the target. CoreMark's\\nrepeated loop backedges and jump sites can reuse resolved targets and\\nrecover those slots even with the existing instruction bus stalls.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Fetch and decode share a cycle in this core. Every taken branch or jump\\ncurren\n6:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261119-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Decouple fetch with one fall-through instruction slot\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The 46.963 MHz champion holds the fetch PC whenever a load-use hazard,\\ndata-memory stall or divide blocks decode. A ready instruction response\\nduring that hold is lost and the same address may face an imem stall\\nwhen decode resumes. The champion also replaces the fetched word with\n…[2338 of 5410 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b79fc4aed4a96ee5]…\nrecovery across the execute boundary\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The bench champion scores 155.40 iter/s at a 61.972 MHz median clock\\nand 3,987,921 timed cycles. Its reported worst path runs 21.371 ns\\nfrom data-memory readback through MEM forwarding and EX recovery\\nto the IF PC register. The prior structural trials show the value\\nof keeping immediate load consumers on the one-cycle issue path.\\nMoving the recovery selection to the output side of the existing\\\n18:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261119-002-r6s1\", \"title\": \"Retime result selection across EX/MEM\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The bench champion scores 169.16 CoreMark iterations per second at\\n67.460 MHz with 3,987,921 marker-bracketed cycles. Its Gowin reports\\nshow both register-file and data-RAM readback reaching the EX/MEM\\ninteger result and BTB write enables on limiting paths. EX currently\\nselects among add/subtract, shift, divide and other integer results\\nbefore registering one integer data word. BTB training similarly\\nqual\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
