{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20261119-00[123]-r[467]s[012]\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c '\nimport json,sys\nfor l in sys.stdin:\n r=json.loads(l)\n print(r[\\\"id\\\"],r[\\\"title\\\"],r[\\\"fmax_mhz\\\"],r[\\\"cycles\\\"],r[\\\"fitness\\\"],r[\\\"lut4\\\"])\n if r[\\\"id\\\"] in (\\\"hyp-20261119-002-r7s1\\\",\\\"hyp-20261119-003-r7s2\\\",\\\"hyp-20261119-001-r7s0\\\"):\n  print(r[\\\"hypothesis\\\"]); print(\\\"critical:\\\",str(r[\\\"critical_path\\\"])[:1800]); print(\\\"implementation:\\\",str(r[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"])[:6200])\n'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261119-001-r4s0 Split RV32M arithmetic across existing register boundaries 60.126 4227639 142.22 3456\nhyp-20261119-002-r4s1 Split execute forwarding into ALU, multiply and load lanes 61.972 3987921 155.4 3211\nhyp-20261119-003-r4s2 Derive queued fetch successors from the BTB request stream 56.469 3959069 142.63 3907\nhyp-20261119-001-r6s0 Move late EX output selection across EX/MEM 71.49 3987921 179.27 3349\nhyp-20261119-002-r6s1 Retime result selection across EX/MEM 73.329 3987921 183.88 3342\nhyp-20261119-003-r6s2 Replay two instruction words at predicted taken targets 64.781 3561615 181.89 3891\nhyp-20261119-001-r7s0 Compute AUIPC in ID to remove the EX operand mux 78.576 3987921 197.03 3387\n{'motivation': 'The bench champion scores 183.88 CoreMark iterations per second at\\n73.329 MHz in 3,987,921 marker-bracketed cycles. In the median Gowin\\nplacements, the path from data RAM through load forwarding and the\\nAUIPC operand mux into the EX/MEM shifter register is within 0.001 ns\\nof the worst fetch-recovery path. Every instruction on the ordinary\\nALU operand A path passes through that mux. AUIPC can compute its\\nresult from the fetch PC and instruction bits before EX because its\\nimmediate has twelve zero low bits and uses no register operand.\\n', 'hypothesis': \"On the existing ID/EX payload capture edge, register the low 20 bits\\nof pc[31:12] + instr[31:12] alongside the PC. In EX, concatenate this\\nsum with the saved pc[11:0] to form the full modulo-2^32 AUIPC result.\\nDrive the ALU A input directly from forwarded rs1. Exclude AUIPC from\\nthe add/subtract result selector and put its value on the existing\\nother-result lane. Give jump links first priority on that lane,\\nAUIPC second, and the ordinary other ALU result third. Register the\\nlane and its existing one-hot control as usual so the immediately\\nfollowing instruction can use AUIPC via the normal EX/MEM bypass.\\nThe new upper sum uses the same hold and squash handling as the PC\\nand other ID/EX payload fields.\\n\\nAn isolated prototype measured 75.559,\n…[16424 of 19496 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:ebaa20e30c62a492]…\nrk passed with both stall streams and no out-of-bounds accesses. The UART reported `Correct operation validated.` and all five canonical CRCs (`e9f5`, `e714`, `1fd7`, `8e3a`, `fcaf`).\n\n## Performance\n\n`make fpga TARGET=bench` measured 3,337,377 marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles for 10 iterations. The starting RTL took 3,987,921 cycles with the same ELF and stall streams, a reduction of 16.31%. The implementation finished 29,715 cycles ahead of the scratch prototype.\n\n| Gowin placement option | Fmax (MHz) |\n| --- | ---: |\n| 0 | 70.632 |\n| 1 | 71.024 |\n| 2 | 71.024 |\n| Median | 71.024 |\n\nOption 0 used 3,698 logic cells, 1,041 registers, and six BSRAMs. Its worst setup path ran from data RAM to the EX shift-result register. The median clears the 64.77 MHz floor and yields 212.81 iterations/s, 15.74% above 183.88. It is 1.934 MHz and 3.87 iterations/s below the scratch prototype's median and projected score. Two equivalent fetch-PC selector rewrites measured lower median Fmax, so the faster selector is retained.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
