{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_2", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n -m 25 '\\\"category\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"structural\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | cut -c1-1200\"", "aggregated_output": "3:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261119-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Share a registered execute-stage divider for DIV and REM\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The bench retest reaches 5.442 MHz and reports a 199.837 ns, 156-cell path through EX forwarding and the ALU to the data-memory path. The ALU feeds its common result mux with four combinational division and remainder expressions. The depth of an unrolled divider makes it the leading suspect for the low clock rate, and the mux exposes its delay even on cycles issuing ordinary instructions. A baseline Verilator retirement trace with both bus stalls enabled and all CoreMark CRCs validated counted zero DIV/REM instructions and 93,960 MUL instructions in the 4,491,485-cycle timed interval.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The bench retest reaches 5.442 MHz and reports a 199.837 ns, 156-cell path through EX forwarding and the ALU to the data-memory path. The ALU feeds its common result mux with four combinational division and remainder expressions. The depth of an unrolled divider makes it the leading suspect for the low clock rate, and the mux exposes its delay even on cycles issuing ordinary instructions. A baseline Verilator retirement trace \n6:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261119-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Decouple fetch with one fall-through instruction slot\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The 46.963 MHz champion holds the fetch PC whenever a load-use hazard,\\ndata-memory stall or divide blocks decode. A ready instruction response\\nduring that hold is lost and the same address may face an imem stall\\nwhen decode resumes. The champion also replaces the fetched word with\\na NOP on EX redirect. Its reported 27.746 ns worst path runs from EX\\nforwarding through that flush and back to the ID/EX JALR decode bit.\\nAn elastic fetch boundary can save a ready response and squash by\\nvalidity, letting decode see the selected instruction word directly.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The 46.963 MHz champion holds the fetch PC whenever a load-use hazard,\\ndata-memory stall or divide blocks dec\n…[6536 of 9608 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:62bfcf8c323a6fe4]…\nark iterations per second at a\\n71.024 MHz median Gowin clock in 3,337,377 marker-bracketed cycles.\\nIts worst reported path runs from data RAM through load forwarding\\nto the EX/MEM shift-result register. The round-seven structural trial\\nreached 79.065 MHz by removing raw load forwarding from the ALU and\\nJALR, but one placement still timed through the dependent memory\\naddress. That round's trace counted 135,320 immediate load-dependent\\nbranches, 59,270 immediate load-dependent loads and roughly 9,400\\nother adjacent load consumers. A new marker-bracketed trace classified\\nall 135,320 immediately dependent branches as BEQ or BNE. Their equality\\ntest can use the formatted memory word through a short bypass while the\\nregistered writeback boundary takes RAM readback out of the arithmetic,\\naddress, store-data and ordered-compare paths.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The bench champion scores 212.81 CoreMark iterations per second at a\\n71.024 MHz median Gowin clock in 3,337,377 marker-bracketed cycles.\\\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_1", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md'", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r1s0 (regression, +673.76%): Replacing combinational DIV/REM with a shared, registered restoring divider moved the critical path from EX-to-memory logic into the six-step divider round, raising median Gowin Fmax from 5.442 to 42.123 MHz while the divide-free CoreMark window avoided the extra EX cycles.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r1s2 (regression, +8.09%): With combinational DIV/REM in EX still limiting the clock to 5.5 MHz, the 16-entry tagged BTB's 6.1% CoreMark cycle savings from reusing taken branch and jump targets under bus stalls were too small to close the throughput gap to the 47.0 MHz registered-divider variant.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r2s0 (regression, +4.61%): Squashing decode through the ID/EX valid bit cut the EX redirect feedback through IF and hazard logic into the ID/EX payload, exposing the five-step divider update as the Gowin timing limit and leaving a placement-sensitive CoreMark gain at 49.128 MHz median Fmax with unchanged cycle count.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-002-r2s1 (regression, +6.19%): A one-entry fall-through fetch slot cut CoreMark cycles by 3.07% by saving ready imem responses during decode holds, while the round's BTB won at a lower median Fmax by avoiding taken-branch and jump bubbles and cutting cycles by 6.68%.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r2s2 (improvement, +9.09%): To pair the BTB's CoreMark cycle savings under bus stalls with a shorter recovery path, precompute full-width direct targets in ID, compare the fetch PC against target and fall-through in parallel with branch resolution, and redirect fetch with a validity-only ID/EX squash.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r3s0 (improvement, +11.45%): Replacing five serial restoring steps with two radix-4 steps using parallel divisor trials and one radix-2 step shortened the divider recurrence, and registering the unmasked JALR sum for load/store addresses bypassed the ALU result mux, together raising median Gowin Fmax from 47.807 to 53.281 MHz and shifting the critical path to the register-file-\n…[5306 of 8378 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:e04a036a57499e4f]…\ne low-byte mux before per-destination load gating shortened the RAM-to-shifter forwarding path and exposed a divider-limited 75.894 MHz Gowin median, with unchanged CoreMark cycles leaving throughput behind the round winner's stall-reducing fetch replay.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-002-r8s1 (regression, +7.91%): Direct formatted-load forwarding to bytewise BEQ/BNE comparators kept all 135,320 immediate load-dependent CoreMark branches bubble-free, while replaying pointer loads and other consumers with MEM/WB forwarding cut the RAM-to-address path at a cycle cost that left throughput behind the round winner's larger instruction replay store.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r8s2 (improvement, +9.33%): Keeping BTB-armed replay eligible until reset or FENCE.I and growing the direct-mapped store from 128 to 1,024 words cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 4.35% by avoiding window expiry and reducing index conflicts, while deriving fills directly from accepted imem responses kept replay lookup off the RAM write-enable path.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
