D/EX validity on holds, with pending BTB writes bypassed into IF and ID/EX to preserve prediction timing.\n14:- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r5s2 (regression, +5.67%): Issuing the corrected imem request during EX/MEM validation and continuing at corrected PC+4 on acceptance keeps BTB lookup off the redirect path, preserving same-cycle recovery with a 0.026% CoreMark cycle increase while RAM readback into the EX/MEM ALU result limits Gowin Fmax.\n15:- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r6s0 (regression, +5.98%): Registering the shift result separately and forming BTB writes from registered training facts in the recovery cycle moved late selection past EX/MEM, raising median Gowin Fmax from 67.46 to 71.49 MHz at unchanged CoreMark cycles and exposing the setup path from data RAM through load formatting and EX forwarding to the multiply-result register.\n23:- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r8s2 (improvement, +9.33%): Keeping BTB-armed replay eligible until reset or FENCE.I and growing the direct-mapped store from 128 to 1,024 words cut stalled CoreMark cycles by 4.35% by avoiding window expiry and reducing index conflicts, while deriving fills directly from accepted imem responses kept replay lookup off the RAM write-enable path.\n25:- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-002-r9s1 (improvement, +7.93%): Ready-LW address and formatted BEQ/BNE bypasses kept CoreMark's dominant load-use pairs bubble-free, while independent redirect acceptance and precomputing the ordinary fetch successor for each BTB training outcome let registered branch resolution feed synchronous replay RAM through a final address select.\n26:- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r9s2 (regression, -7.43%): Packing validity into a scrubbed 4K-word BTB-armed replay RAM saved 1,801 logic cells and used seven more BSRAMs, with the 0.28% stalled CoreMark cycle savings too small to offset the median Gowin Fmax drop from 74.271 to 68.560 MHz while the data RAM path into the EX shifter limited timing.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
