{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"id\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"hyp-20261119-002-r9s1\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import json,sys; r=json.load(sys.stdin); print({k:r[k] for k in (\\\"cycles\\\",\\\"fitness\\\",\\\"seeds\\\",\\\"critical_path\\\",\\\"logic_levels\\\")}); print(r[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"])'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"id\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"hyp-20261119-002-r9s1\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import json,sys; r=json.load(sys.stdin); print({k:r[k] for k in (\\\"cycles\\\",\\\"fitness\\\",\\\"seeds\\\",\\\"critical_path\\\",\\\"logic_levels\\\")}); print(r[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"])'\"","aggregated_output":"{'cycles': 3202739, 'fitness': 251.12, 'seeds': [81.13, 80.427, 80.427], 'critical_path': {'from': 'cpu/u_if/normal_pc_q_11_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_if/replay_valid_slots_replay_valid_slots_0_0_s', 'slack_ns': -7.326, 'arrival_ns': 17.62, 'logic_cells': 16, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_if']}, 'logic_levels': 13}\n# Split live load consumers at the execute boundary\n\nID predecodes the architectural source operands and retains both raw register fields for RVFI. EX keeps most consumers on register-backed forwarding, with a ready LW supplying the raw base for a dependent load and formatted load data supplying BEQ and BNE. The equality lane registers four byte predicates. Recovery reduces them and selects between the BTB counter candidates computed in EX. The single RVFI channel still retires at MEM/WB.\n\nWhen another consumer needs the live load, the load's accepting edge inserts one EX/MEM bubble. ID/EX retains the consumer, saves its other forwarded operands, and moves the load matches to MEM/WB. Older data-memory backpressure holds the request and its forwarding state. Divider launch, memory side effects, and BTB training are qualified by the corresponding instruction's advance.\n\nOrdinary fetch acceptance uses the unsquashed ID/EX candidate. The ordinary-PC BTB lookup computes both pending-training successors before the registered branch predicates select one. A corrected request can accept on the recovery beat using imem readiness and the older memory hold. On that beat it advances sequentially, and its metadata lookup uses the captured PC and the committed older BTB update. When the correction cannot accept on the recovery beat, its PC becomes the ordinary request for retry.\n\nReplay validity now uses 32 resettable bank flags and RAM-backed bitmaps to shorten lookup and update paths. The fetch bitmap read, replay word read, and PC register use the same selected next PC. A registered write bypass covers both bitmap read ports on a same-bank collision. FENCE.I clears all bank flags in one edge. The first subsequent fill in a bank replaces its old bitmap. The replay word checks its full tag and excludes a sampled read/write collision.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- The requested Verilator lint command passed with no warnings.\n- `make test TARGET=bench` passed all 132 cocotb cases, including 61 pipeline cases. Directed cases cover full-width LW chains, byte and halfword equality, signed ordering, raw unused operand values, and immediate corrections under memory and instruction stalls.\n- `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench` passed selftest and all three random ELFs with and without bus stalls. CoreMark reported the canonical CRCs and its validation message.\n- `CORE_NAME=bench RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl bash formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 ALTOPS checks with the single-channel wrapper.\n- `NRET=1 make fpga TARGET=bench` measured 81.130, 80.427, and 80.427 MHz across the three Gowin placements. CoreMark completed ten iterations in 3,202,739 marker-bracketed cycles, yielding 251.12 iterations/s at the 80.427 MHz median.\n\nThe replay address path limits both placements at the median. The divider recurrence, ordered comparison, and register-backed shift paths are close to that limit. Compared with the starting core, the timed workload uses 10,630 more cycles.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
