{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_35","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20261119-002-r10s1\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import json,sys; r=json.load(sys.stdin); print(r[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"]); print(r[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"])'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_35", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20261119-002-r10s1\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import json,sys; r=json.load(sys.stdin); print(r[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"]); print(r[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"])'\"", "aggregated_output": "Split fetch into a successor address path and a shared clock enable for\nthe ordinary PC and the single synchronous replay read. Form the ordinary\npredicted successor, including same-index BTB training substitutions and\nlast-word parking, independently of ordinary acceptance. Select that\nsuccessor or the corrected continuation as the read address. On reset,\nredirect or ordinary acceptance, sample the address into the PC and\nreplay read together. Otherwise hold both. Replay hit and backpressure\ndrive the enable while the successor address is computed in parallel.\nA correction still presents the full corrected PC immediately and uses\nthe existing ready-bus and older MEM hold rules for same-beat recovery.\n\nPack validity with the full replay tag and instruction in the existing\n1,024-entry RAM. This lets the held read retain all of its metadata in\none place. On each enabled read, also capture the concurrent write's\nindex, validity, tag and instruction. On physical index equality use\nthat captured write record, with the full PC tag and range checks, in\nplace of the RAM output. Hold the collision record when the read holds.\nThis makes a same-index replacement visible independent of the RAM's\nread-during-write mode. A different-tag collision still misses. Raw\nready imem data takes priority and only accepted ready beats fill.\n\nReset and advancing FENCE.I invalidate the read and disarm replay\nimmediately, then scrub all 1,024 valid bits through the write port.\nA new invalidation restarts the sweep. Keep issuing ready bus words\nwhile scrubbing and reject any held read sampled during the sweep after\nit ends. Resume filling after the sweep under the existing BTB arming\nrules. At ID/EX capture payload on !hold or squash_candidate while using\nthe existing fetch_accept to qualify validity. This keeps the replay-hit\npath out of the wide payload enable and preserves load-replay retention,\nsquash priority and nret=1 retirement.\n\nA scratch implementation measured 76.608, 84.297 and 84.297 MHz across\nthe three Gowin placements, using \n…[2013 of 5085 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:df9a7cc30dfc57b5]…\n instructions through startup and post-fence sweeps. They exercise matching and conflicting write collisions, refresh by ready data, immediate exclusion of stale words after FENCE.I, and a repeated fence whose restarted sweep ends while a stale read is held.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint, the RVFI channel-0 port check, cocotb, and single-channel cosim passed. Cosim covered selftest and three random traces with and without stalls, plus stalled CoreMark with canonical CRCs, no out-of-bounds accesses, and 3,202,555 marker-bracketed cycles.\n- The unchanged single-channel `formal/run_all.sh` gate passed all 53 checks on the selected implementation. The depth-20 register proof completed in about 16 minutes.\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 measured 77.239, 80.541, and 80.541 MHz, using 4,117 logic cells and seven BSRAMs. The median corresponds to 251.49 CoreMark iterations/s, up 0.15% from 251.12. The limiting path is in the divider, and the improvement falls short of the 4.6% promotion threshold.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
