{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg '\\\"id\\\": \\\"hyp-20261119-00[123]-r(10|11)s[012]\\\"' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python3 -c 'import json,sys; [print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",x[\\\"id\\\"],x[\\\"title\\\"],x[\\\"cycles\\\"],x[\\\"fmax_mhz\\\"],x[\\\"seeds\\\"],x[\\\"critical_path\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",x[\\\"hypothesis\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",x[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"][:5000]) for x in map(json.loads,sys.stdin)]'\"", "aggregated_output": "\n hyp-20261119-001-r10s0 Select fetch successors with one-hot enables 3202739 83.372 [78.134, 83.372, 83.372] {'from': 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu/divisor_q_0_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu/partial_q_35_s1', 'slack_ns': -7.798, 'arrival_ns': 18.093, 'logic_cells': 66, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu']} \n {'motivation': 'The current champion runs CoreMark in 3,202,739 cycles at a median\\n80.427 MHz. Both median Gowin placements trace the limiting path from\\nnormal_pc_q[11] through the same-bank validity bypass, slot lookup,\\nfetch acceptance and successor muxes to the synchronous replay address.\\nThe bank match currently selects all 32 bits before the slot lookup,\\nand ordinary acceptance controls multiple successive PC muxes.\\n', 'hypothesis': \"Compute the two candidate validity bits independently from the RAM\\noutput and the last write bitmap. Let the late bank comparison select\\nbetween those two bits. Keep the full bitmap bypass on the write side,\\nwhere consecutive fills in a bank accumulate prior slot bits.\\n\\nReplace the successive hold and prediction PC muxes with a single\\nmasked OR. Generate mutually exclusive enables for the held PC, PC+4,\\nthe BTB table target, the pending write target, the corrected target,\\nand the corrected fall-through. Use the existing training-outcome\\npredicates for the table and pending target enables. Select the held PC\\nwhen ordinary fetch cannot accept or is at the last word without a\\ntaken prediction. Each correction uses its existing corrected acceptance\\nto select the corrected PC or its increment. Feed the same selected PC\\nto the PC register, replay word read, and replay bitmap read.\\n\\nA scratch prototype measured 78.264, 81.474, and 81.474 MHz across\\nGowin placement options 0, 1, and 2. CoreMark validated and retained\\nthe champion's 3,202,739 marker-bracketed cycles. The divider recurrence\\nbecame critical in the median placements. The measured fitness was\\n254.39 iterations/s, a 1.3% gain. Promotion at the same cycle count\\nrequires a median greater than 84.1266 MHz.\\n\"\n…[29799 of 32871 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:0dba2a3e5abeca75]…\ndecodes the slot in parallel and selects one bit from each of the existing sources before the same-bank bypass. This was a timing change to the slot selector. The replay reset, FENCE.I, stall, and collision behavior is preserved. Cocotb now checks recovery during clear and committed prediction during a pending same-index replacement. Tests that depend on a trained entry hold the first imem response through the clear.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint and all five cocotb suites passed.\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-retirement checks on the final RTL with zero failures and zero retries.\n- Co-simulation passed for five ELFs. Stalled CoreMark reported all five canonical CRCs and `Correct operation validated.` in 3,202,739 bracketed cycles.\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 measured 78.287, 79.456, and 79.456 MHz. The 79.456 MHz median projects 248.09 iterations/s and falls below the 90.75 MHz qualification threshold. The divider recurrence was the worst path in all three placements.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
