{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_56", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"ls -l cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-003-r1s2.yaml && sed -n '1,150p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-003-r1s2.yaml\"", "aggregated_output": "-rw-rw----+ 1 hwebench2 hwebench2 4095 Nov 19 15:17 cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-003-r1s2.yaml\nid: hyp-20261119-003-r1s2\ntitle: \"Predict direct branches with a 16-entry tagged BTB\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Fetch and decode share a cycle in this core. Every taken branch or jump\n  currently discards one fetch slot while EX supplies the target. CoreMark's\n  repeated loop backedges and direct transfers can reuse resolved targets and\n  recover those slots even with the existing instruction bus stalls.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a 16-entry direct-mapped branch target buffer for conditional branches\n  and JAL. Index by PC[5:2] and match PC[19:6] for aligned fetch PCs below\n  1 MiB. Each entry holds an aligned in-range target as an 18-bit word\n  address and a two-bit saturating direction counter. On a taken miss,\n  allocate at weakly taken. On a matching conditional branch, train the\n  counter toward its resolved direction and refresh the target when taken.\n  Train JAL as strongly taken. Keep sequential fetch on misses and on hits\n  whose counter predicts fall-through. This places a tag check and a mux on\n  the registered fetch PC path and uses the stored target directly.\n\n  Apply the prediction only when IF/ID is accepted into ID/EX. During the\n  following EX cycle, the fetch PC is already that instruction's predicted\n  successor and holds there if an older data access stalls EX. On a valid\n  EX advance, compare that PC with the full resolved architectural next PC\n  for every instruction. On a mismatch, redirect and squash the younger\n  instruction, even when instruction memory is unready. Train once on EX\n  advance using the hit and counter captured when the instruction was\n  accepted. If the same acceptance edge writes the fetched index, bypass\n  the written tag and counter into the captured training state. Keep fetch\n  prediction on the stored entry so that the bypass only feeds training.\n  This uses one lookup per cycle and preserves saturation on adjacent\n  updates. Preserve EX's misalignment\n…[1137 of 4209 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:6e825b7f3dee4b96]…\nription: >-\n      Use the resolved next PC for both RVFI and recovery. Compare it with the\n      current fetch PC for every valid advancing instruction, including\n      fall-through and trap cases. Export the resolving PC, direction and\n      target for training only on a valid advancing branch or JAL with no\n      target fault. Supply the target for allocation only on aligned, in-range\n      taken transfers from aligned, in-range source PCs.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: >-\n      Wire the registered fetch PC into EX and route EX training to IF. Route\n      the advance-qualified mismatch to the existing hazard and flush logic\n      so a data-memory hold defers recovery and an instruction-memory stall\n      still permits it.\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: >-\n      Drive the data-memory hold outputs independently of the redirect and\n      load-use block so EX can gate recovery and training with its existing\n      stall input. Keep the data-memory hold ahead of younger instruction flush.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
