struction. On a mismatch, redirect and squash the younger\\ninstruction, even when instruction memory is unready. Train once on EX\\nadvance using the hit and counter captured when the instruction was\\naccepted. If the same acceptance edge writes the fetched index, compare\\nthe fetch tag against the written tag for the ca\n…[4130 of 7202 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:fb1388dbd4ef249c]…\nagainst fall-through and the\\napplicable target in parallel with branch direction resolution, then\\nselect the match for the resolved outcome. JALR uses the forwarded base\\nand full I-immediate, and clears bit zero of their sum. On any valid\\nEX advance, recover on a mismatch, including a stale taken hit on a\\nnon-control instruction, a changed JALR target or an alignment trap\\nfalling through. Use the full architectural next PC for recovery and\\nRVFI.\\n\\nFeed raw imem instruction bits to ID and hazard detection. A\\nmisprediction steers the fetch PC and clears the younger ID/EX valid\\nbit. Only the older-stage hold controls the ID/EX payload enable.\\nQualify load-use detection with a valid load and a ready fetch. Clear\\nside-effect and forwarding controls when EX/MEM captures an invalid\\nslot or a divider wait bubble. An older dmem stall holds the fetch PC,\\nID/EX and EX/MEM, retains the MEM/WB forwarding data and defers recovery\\nand training. An unfinished divide holds the front end. Once EX can\\nadvance, \n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md schemas/hypothesis.schema.json && ls cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses'","aggregated_output":"- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r1s0 (regression, +673.76%): Replacing combinational DIV/REM with a shared, registered restoring divider moved the critical path from EX-to-memory logic into the six-step divider round, raising median Gowin Fmax from 5.442 to 42.123 MHz while the divide-free CoreMark window avoided the extra EX cycles.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r1s2 (regression, +8.09%): With combinational DIV/REM in EX still limiting the clock to 5.5 MHz, the 16-entry tagged BTB's 6.1% CoreMark cycle savings from reusing taken branch and jump targets under bus stalls were too small to close the throughput gap to the 47.0 MHz registered-divider variant.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r2s0 (regression, +4.61%): Squashing decode through the ID/EX valid bit cut the EX redirect feedback through IF and hazard logic into the ID/EX payload, exposing the five-step divider update as the Gowin timing limit and leaving a placement-sensitive CoreMark gain at 49.128 MHz median Fmax with unchanged cycle count.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-002-r2s1 (regression, +6.19%): A one-entry fall-through fetch slot cut CoreMark cycles by 3.07% by saving ready imem responses during decode holds, while the round's BTB won at a lower median Fmax by avoiding taken-branch and jump bubbles and cutting cycles by 6.68%.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r2s2 (improvement, +9.09%): To pair the BTB's CoreMark cycle savings under bus stalls with a shorter recovery path, precompute full-width direct targets in ID, compare the fetch PC against target and fall-through in parallel with branch resolution, and redirect fetch with a validity-only ID/EX squash.\n{\n  \"$schema\": \"http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#\",\n  \"type\": \"object\",\n  \"required\": [\"id\",\"title\",\"category\",