{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_4", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor s in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(s)\n if d.get('category')=='predictor' or d.get('id')=='hyp-20261119-001-r3s0':\n  print(d.keys()); print(json.dumps(d,indent=2)[:11000])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "dict_keys(['id', 'title', 'category', 'motivation', 'hypothesis', 'expected_impact', 'changes', 'outcome', 'fitness', 'delta_pct', 'vs_baseline', 'fmax_mhz', 'ipc_coremark', 'cycles', 'iterations', 'lut4', 'ff', 'seeds', 'perturbations', 'lutram', 'bsram', 'dsp', 'critical_path', 'logic_levels', 'formal_passed', 'cosim_passed', 'error', 'implementation_notes', 'timestamp', 'slot', 'round_id', 'lesson'])\n{\n  \"id\": \"hyp-20261119-003-r1s2\",\n  \"title\": \"Predict branches and jumps with a 16-entry tagged BTB\",\n  \"category\": \"predictor\",\n  \"motivation\": \"Fetch and decode share a cycle in this core. Every taken branch or jump\\ncurrently discards one fetch slot while EX supplies the target. CoreMark's\\nrepeated loop backedges and jump sites can reuse resolved targets and\\nrecover those slots even with the existing instruction bus stalls.\\n\",\n  \"hypothesis\": {\n    \"motivation\": \"Fetch and decode share a cycle in this core. Every taken branch or jump\\ncurrently discards one fetch slot while EX supplies the target. CoreMark's\\nrepeated loop backedges and jump sites can reuse resolved targets and\\nrecover those slots even with the existing instruction bus stalls.\\n\",\n    \"hypothesis\": \"Add a 16-entry direct-mapped branch target buffer for conditional branches\\nand both jump opcodes, JAL and JALR. Index by PC[5:2] and match PC[19:6]\\nfor aligned fetch PCs below 1 MiB. Each entry holds an 18-bit target word\\naddress and a two-bit saturating direction counter. On a taken miss,\\nallocate at weakly taken. On a matching conditional branch, train the\\ncounter toward its resolved direction and refresh the target when taken.\\nFor either jump opcode, store the last resolved target as strongly taken.\\nKeep sequential fetch on misses and on hits whose counter predicts\\nfall-through. This puts a tag check and a mux on the fetch PC path.\\n\\nApply the prediction only when IF/ID is accepted into ID/EX. During the\\nfollowing EX cycle, the fetch PC is already that instruction's predicted\\nsuccessor and holds there if an older data access stall\n…[39613 of 42685 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:35d5cd9bffc52e2a]…\nPC, including the same-index EX update bypass. The target array is read by the request PC. EX qualifies training and recovery with instruction advance. FIFO occupancy determines validity after a flush, and only occupied entries shift on a dequeue.\\n\\nDirected cocotb coverage includes predicted target prefetch during a dmem stall, full-queue refill, a queued load-use dependency with imem unready, wrong-path store squash on dmem release, FENCE.I refetch after a code store, and an in-range redirect from the last RAM word while fetch runs ahead of a divide.\\n\\n## Local verification\\n\\n- Verilator lint passed with `-Wall`. All five cocotb runners passed, including 23 pipeline tests.\\n- Local `bash formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-retire ALTOPS checks on the final RTL. The unchanged harness ran from a scratch copy under `$TMPDIR`. Zero retries were needed for a formal failure.\\n- Full RVFI cosimulation passed for `selftest.elf` and `random1.elf` through `random3.elf` with and without bus stalls.\\n- CoreMark\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
