{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_61", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-003-r4s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261119-003-r4s2\ntitle: \"Derive queued fetch successors from the BTB request stream\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Replay of the existing 32-entry BTB estimated 57,035 wrong successors\n  over 4,191,341 CoreMark cycles. With one slot lost per redirect, better\n  direction accuracy has limited upside. The prior four-entry predicted\n  fetch queue reclaimed 232,272 cycles by issuing buffered instructions\n  through imem stalls and following predictions through decode holds. It\n  reached 49.095 MHz on the earlier arithmetic datapath. The current core\n  reaches 53.281 MHz, so preserving clock speed matters as much as retaining\n  those cycle savings. The prior queue stored the same address twice, as\n  a packet's predicted successor and as the following packet's fetch PC.\nhypothesis: |\n  Run the existing BTB independently of ID issue with a four-entry\n  fall-through queue with four packed 64-bit registers holding the full\n  PC and instruction. Use fixed bit slices for both fields so the formal\n  Yosys frontend reads the same queue as Gowin and Verilator. Advance the\n  request PC along the predicted path on every ready response that enters\n  the queue or bypasses into ID. The head's predicted successor is slot\n  one's PC when a second packet is present and the registered request PC\n  when the head is the only packet. An empty queue bypasses the current\n  response and its BTB-selected successor. These addresses describe the\n  path actually taken at fetch time even if the predictor has since been\n  trained. Keep the oldest packet at a fixed head and support simultaneous\n  dequeue and refill at full occupancy. Insert a new response at the old\n  occupancy minus the dequeue count. This removes 128 bits of queue\n  payload and their shift and refill logic compared with the prior design.\n\n  Carry just the issued successor to EX and retain the parallel full-width\n  comparisons against fall-through and the resolved target. Use a separate\n  validity path for squashing ID/EX. Flush the entire queue on EX recovery\n  and a\n…[2246 of 5318 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:a384218263156135]…\ncted-head validity, ID consumption and EX recovery to IF. Retain the single-channel RVFI and memory interfaces.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Determine load-use and ID issue from the selected packet's validity and instruction fields, including when an occupied queue serves ID while imem is unready.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Capture the issued packet's successor and FENCE.I decode. Keep payload capture independent of the validity-only squash.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Validate the recorded prediction on EX advance and flush on a mismatch, an invalid prediction or an advancing FENCE.I. Preserve full-width recovery, architectural next PC, trap handling and qualified BTB updates.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Cover implicit-successor selection through occupancy transitions, training after a packet was fetched, boundary recovery and wrong-path side-effect suppression across bus stalls. Check ordered RVFI and exactly-once memory effects.\"\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
