{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_86", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,145p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261119-003-r7s2.yaml && git status --short --untracked-files=all\"", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261119-003-r7s2\ntitle: Read BTB target replay one fetch ahead\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The 32-entry BTB already predicts most CoreMark successors. The previous\n  two-word target replay cut timed cycles by 10.69%, but its 64.781 MHz\n  median left it below the 183.88 iter/s champion. A wider window covers\n  more recurrent target instructions. Reading the replay word one cycle\n  ahead gives the decoder a registered word and keeps the replay store off\n  the same-cycle path from EX recovery to the register file.\nhypothesis: |\n  Arm a 32-instruction replay window whenever the BTB selects a taken\n  successor, or registered EX recovery installs a taken successor. Start\n  the window at the selected target, decrement its count only on ID\n  acceptance of a sequential word, and restart it on every new taken\n  edge. Use a 128-word direct-mapped replay RAM indexed by PC[8:2] with\n  packed PC[19:9] tags and instruction words, and separate resettable\n  valid bits. Read the RAM synchronously using the same next PC that\n  enters the ordinary fetch PC register. On an ordinary hold the read\n  stays at that PC. Fill on accepted ready responses at replay misses\n  within active windows and aligned PCs below 1 MiB. Treat the corrected\n  request in the recovery cycle as a miss for filling. Register the\n  write strobe and index alongside the RAM read. In the following cycle\n  compare the registered write index with the registered ordinary PC\n  index and suppress the hit on a collision, independently of RAM\n  read-during-write behavior.\n\n  Keep the presented fetch PC on the instruction bus in both cases.\n  On recovery use the corrected address and accept its instruction only\n  if imem is ready. On a hold, retain that corrected PC and align the\n  registered replay read to it for the next cycle. Select the ID\n  instruction payload using raw imem readiness alone, with ready imem\n  data taking priority over the registered RAM data. Qualify ID validity\n  and fetch advance separately. A replay word can advance only on a\n  fu\n…[1071 of 4143 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:e789a79bde0d641f]…\nonfidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: Add the synchronous tagged replay RAM and BTB-armed window counter. Read the next ordinary PC, compare the sampled write index with the current ordinary PC index, and gate ID payload on raw imem readiness alone. Handle recovery, the last RAM word and FENCE.I.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Pass raw imem readiness into IF and route IF word validity to ID and fetch advance. Preserve existing memory and single-channel RVFI ports.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: Carry an advancing FENCE.I refetch bit in the registered EX resolution record.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: Mark an advancing FENCE.I for forced fall-through recovery and replay invalidation while retaining full-width successor validation and qualified BTB training.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: Add directed coverage for replay with imem unready, tag conflicts, recovered targets across holds, RAM-boundary behavior and FENCE.I refetch.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
