{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -i -n -o 'conflict|capacity|replay.hit|working.set|trace|window|hit.rate' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | cut -c 1-100 | tail -60\npython - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n    r=json.loads(line)\n    if r['id'] in ('hyp-20261119-003-r6s2','hyp-20261119-003-r7s2'):\n        for k in ('id','fitness','cycles','fmax_mhz','lut4','lutram','bsram','critical_path','implementation_notes'):\n            print(k, r.get(k))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "2:trace\n2:window\n2:trace\n2:window\n2:window\n3:trace\n3:trace\n3:window\n3:trace\n11:trace\n11:trace\n12:trace\n12:trace\n14:trace\n14:trace\n15:trace\n21:trace\n21:trace\n22:window\n22:window\n22:window\n22:window\n22:window\n22:window\n22:window\n22:conflict\n22:window\n22:window\n22:window\n22:window\n22:window\n22:window\nid hyp-20261119-003-r6s2\nfitness 181.89\ncycles 3561615\nfmax_mhz 64.781\nlut4 3891\nlutram 54\nbsram 6\ncritical_path {'from': 'dmem_0_dmem_0_0_0_s', 'to': 'cpu/u_ex/reg_q.alu_result_20_s0', 'slack_ns': -10.914, 'arrival_ns': 21.209, 'logic_cells': 14, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_mem', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']}\nimplementation_notes \nid hyp-20261119-003-r7s2\nfitness 212.81\ncycles 3337377\nfmax_mhz 71.024\nlut4 3698\nlutram 58\nbsram 6\ncritical_path {'from': 'dmem_2_dmem_2_0_0_s', 'to': 'cpu/u_ex/reg_q.shift_result_24_s0', 'slack_ns': -9.158, 'arrival_ns': 19.452, 'logic_cells': 13, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_mem', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_mem', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu']}\nimplementation_notes # Read BTB target replay one fetch ahead\n\nIF reads a 128-word direct-mapped replay RAM at the same edge that registers the next ordinary fetch PC. Each entry stores PC[19:9] and the instruction. A resettable bitmap tracks validity. The registered fill strobe and index suppress a same-index hit on the following cycle, independent of the RAM's read-during-write mode.\n\nA taken BTB prediction or a taken EX correction starts a 32-word window. An accepted corrected word consumes its first slot. If that word selects another taken successor, the window starts at the newly selected target. Recovery drives the corrected address and requires a ready bus response for acceptance. When recovery holds, IF registers the corrected address and reads replay at that address. The instruction mux selects its payload using raw imem readiness. IF qualifies acceptance separately with the replay tag, range, validity, collision, and pipeline hold checks.\n\nThe IF logic now supplies word validity directly to ID. The hazard unit retains the data-memory and divider hold logic. EX carries a one-cycle FENCE.I refetch bit and ignores parked addresses in its successor comparisons. This lets a jump at the last RAM word recover even when it targets that same word. FENCE.I forces a fall-through refetch, clears replay validity, and cancels the old window.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed with `-Wall`.\n- `formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 single-retire ALTOPS checks. No counterexamples or correctness retries.\n- All five cocotb suites passed, including 38 pipeline cases. New cases cover 32 accepted words, ready imem priority, tag and fill collisions, recovery holds, FENCE.I after an instruction store, and last-word parking with both a full-width link and a self-jump.\n- `make cosim TARGET=bench` passed selftest and all three random programs with and without stalls. CoreMark passed with both stall streams and no out-of-bounds accesses. The UART reported `Correct operation validated.` and all five canonical CRCs (`e9f5`, `e714`, `1fd7`, `8e3a`, `fcaf`).\n\n## Performance\n\n`make fpga TARGET=bench` measured 3,337,377 marker-bracketed CoreMark cycles for 10 iterations. The starting RTL took 3,987,921 cycles with the same ELF and stall streams, a reduction of 16.31%. The implementation finished 29,715 cycles ahead of the scratch prototype.\n\n| Gowin placement option | Fmax (MHz) |\n| --- | ---: |\n| 0 | 70.632 |\n| 1 | 71.024 |\n| 2 | 71.024 |\n| Median | 71.024 |\n\nOption 0 used 3,698 logic cells, 1,041 registers, and six BSRAMs. Its worst setup path ran from data RAM to the EX shift-result register. The median clears the 64.77 MHz floor and yields 212.81 iterations/s, 15.74% above 183.88. It is 1.934 MHz and 3.87 iterations/s below the scratch prototype's median and projected score. Two equivalent fetch-PC selector rewrites measured lower median Fmax, so the faster selector is retained.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
