ted upside. The prior four-entry predicted\\nfetch queue reclaimed 232,272 cycles by issuing buffered instructions\\nthrough imem stall\n16:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261119-003-r5s2\", \"title\": \"Validate BTB predictions across the EX/MEM boundary\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The 155.40 iter/s champion runs 3,987,921 marker-bracketed CoreMark\\ncycles at 61.972 MHz. The reported worst Gowin path starts at data RAM,\\npasses through load forwarding and EX, and ends at the fetch PC. The\\nprior predictor replay estimated 57,035 wrong successors in 4,191,341\\ncycles. Registering the EX f\n19:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261119-003-r6s2\", \"title\": \"Replay two instruction words at predicted taken targets\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The champion's BTB predicts the next address, but every correctly\\npredicted taken transfer still waits for the instruction bus to supply\\nthe first word at that address. CoreMark repeatedly returns to hot loop\\nentries and function targets. A small branch target instruction cache\\ncan replay the first two\n22:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261119-003-r7s2\", \"title\": \"Read BTB target replay one fetch ahead\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The 32-entry BTB already predicts most CoreMark successors. The previous\\ntwo-word target replay cut timed cycles by 10.69%, but its 64.781 MHz\\nmedian left it below the 183.88 iter/s champion. A wider window covers\\nmore recurrent target instructions. Reading the replay word one cycle\\nahead gives the decoder a registered\n25:{\"id\": \"hyp-20261119-003-r8s2\", \"title\": \"Keep BTB-armed instruction replay active in a larger store\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"The current 32-word BTB-armed replay window improved the timed CoreMark run\\nfrom 3,987,921 to 3,337,377 cycles at a 71.024 MHz median. The backing store\\nholds 128 instructions, so stream PCs 512 bytes apart can evict each\\nother. The window also expires after 32 accepted words on long paths\\nthrough r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_3", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md && cat schemas/hypothesis.schema.json'", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r1s0 (regression, +673.76%): Replacing combinational DIV/REM with a shared, registered restoring divider moved the critical path from EX-to-memory logic into the six-step divider round, raising median Gowin Fmax from 5.442 to 42.123 MHz while the divide-free CoreMark window avoided the extra EX cycles.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r1s2 (regression, +8.09%): With combinational DIV/REM in EX still limiting the clock to 5.5 MHz, the 16-entry tagged BTB's 6.1% CoreMark cycle savings from reusing taken branch and jump targets under bus stalls were too small to close the throughput gap to the 47.0 MHz registered-divider variant.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r2s0 (regression, +4.61%): Squashing decode through the ID/EX valid bit cut the EX redirect feedback through IF and hazard logic into the ID/EX payload, exposing the five-step divider update as the Gowin timing limit and leaving a placement-sensitive CoreMark gain at 49.128 MHz median Fmax with unchanged cycle count.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-002-r2s1 (regression, +6.19%): A one-entry fall-through fetch slot cut CoreMark cycles by 3.07% by saving ready imem responses during decode holds, while the round's BTB won at a lower median Fmax by avoiding taken-branch and jump bubbles and cutting cycles by 6.68%.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-003-r2s2 (improvement, +9.09%): To pair the BTB's CoreMark cycle savings under bus stalls with a shorter recovery path, precompute full-width direct targets in ID, compare the fetch PC against target and fall-through in parallel with branch resolution, and redirect fetch with a validity-only ID/EX squash.\n- 2026-11-19 hyp-20261119-001-r3s0 (improvement, +11.45%): Replacing five serial restoring steps with two radix-4 steps using parallel divisor trials and one ra