{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_28", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(line)\n if d['id'].endswith(('r7s1','r8s1','r8s0')):\n  print(d['id'], *(d[k] for k in ('cycles','fmax_mhz','lut4','fitness','title')))\n  print(d['hypothesis'])\n  print((d['implementation_notes'] or '')[:7100])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261119-002-r7s1 3995132 79.065 4195 197.9 Route immediate load forwarding through branch and address lanes\n{'motivation': 'The bench champion scores 183.88 CoreMark iter/s at 73.329 MHz in\\n3,987,921 marker-bracketed cycles. A retirement trace counted\\n135,320 branches and 59,270 loads that immediately follow a load\\nwhose result they use. It counted about 9,400 other adjacent load\\nconsumers. Routing every load result into the common EX operands\\nputs RAM readback on the arithmetic and fetch recovery paths.\\nCutting every direct load path reached 77.473 MHz in scratch but\\nraised the cycle count to 4,147,415. A split operand path can\\npreserve the load-use timing of the two common cases.\\n', 'hypothesis': \"Build two EX operand paths from the existing one-hot bypass lanes.\\nRoute formatted MEM load data only to the branch comparators,\\neffective-address adder and store-data path. Feed integer ALU,\\nmultiply, divider and a separate JALR target adder from the\\nregister-backed lanes. For a live instruction on that path whose\\nyoungest eligible producer is a load, hold ID/EX and fetch. On\\nthe edge when the older load is accepted, insert one EX/MEM bubble,\\nretain the forwarded value of each other operand in ID/EX and\\npromote the matching load dependencies to MEM/WB. The consumer\\nexecutes the following cycle with the registered load result.\\nBackpressure keeps the request and original forwarding sources\\nstable until acceptance. Clear the younger ALU and divider side\\neffects during a replay wait