{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_66", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor line in Path('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl').read_text().splitlines():\n    x=json.loads(line)\n    if 'r9s2' in x['id']:\n        print(x['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\n        print(x['implementation_notes'][:5500])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "Expand the predictor-armed instruction replay store to 4,096 words and\npack each entry's valid bit, full PC[19:14] tag and 32-bit instruction\ninto one 39-bit RAM word. Index by PC[13:2]. Keep the synchronous read\naligned to the next ordinary fetch PC so validity arrives with the\nword. Use a sequential scrub on reset and each advancing FENCE.I to\nwrite invalid entries through the existing RAM write port. Drive the\ntag and payload directly from the bus-side fill signals on every\nwrite, since the scrub only needs to write validity to zero. Gate all\nreplay hits while the scrub is active, starting in the invalidation\ncycle. Restart the scrub at zero on each invalidation and enable hits\nonly after the last write completes. Fetch and retire through the\nnormal imem path while scrubbing. Record taken prediction or taken\nrecovery arming during the scrub and resume fills once it completes.\n\nGive scrub writes priority over fills. Otherwise preserve refresh\nfrom accepted ready imem responses with a fill enable independent of\nreplay hit. Carry the sampled write index and strobe alongside the\nread to reject read/write collisions across all twelve index bits.\nKeep exact aligned in-range PC qualification, bus-first acceptance,\nrecovery and parking behavior, and full-width EX validation. FENCE.I\nstill forces a fall-through refetch and clears the arm with priority.\n\nScratch Gowin placements reached 68.623, 74.642 and 74.642 MHz,\nusing 3,482 logic cells, 926 registers and 14 BSRAMs in option 0.\nThis is 1,767 fewer logic cells and seven more BSRAMs than the\nchampion. The reported median-placement bottleneck remained data RAM\nto the EX shifter. Scratch CoreMark took 3,183,041 marker-bracketed\ncycles with the canonical CRCs, giving 234.50 iter/s, up 0.79%.\nPromotion at these cycles requires a median above 77.47 MHz, so the\nmeasured result leaves a timing gap of 2.83 MHz. The prototype matched\nthe reference RVFI traces on selftest and all three random programs\nwith and without stalls. Two directed cases passed for replay after\nstartup a\n…[1846 of 4918 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:1b0eab90734d79ab]…\n\n  passed, including the 50 pipeline cases.\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 checks with the configured\n  single-retire wrapper and zero retries. The bounded formal checks\n  cover bus operation during startup. The directed pipeline tests\n  exercise replay at and after scrub completion.\n- Co-simulation matched selftest and all three random programs against\n  the reference with and without stalls. Stalled CoreMark produced the\n  canonical CRCs and validated operation.\n- Gowin mapped replay to 10 BSRAMs. Option 0 used 3,448 logic cells,\n  926 registers, and 14 BSRAMs total. Placement options 0, 1, and 2\n  reached 66.153, 68.560, and 68.560 MHz, respectively. Option 0 was\n  limited by EX recovery to the replay read address. Options 1 and 2\n  were limited by data RAM to the EX shifter.\n- CoreMark took 3,183,041 marker-bracketed cycles across 10 iterations,\n  9,068 fewer cycles than the champion. The 68.560 MHz median yields\n  215.39 iterations/s and is 8.910 MHz below the 77.47 MHz promotion\n  threshold.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
