{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg 'hyp-20261119-003-r10s2|hyp-20261119-002-r12s1|hyp-20261119-003-r12s2|hyp-20261119-00[123]-r13s[012]|hyp-20261119-002-r11s1' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json,sys; rows=list(map(json.loads,sys.stdin)); [print(x[\\\"id\\\"],x[\\\"fmax_mhz\\\"],x[\\\"cycles\\\"],x[\\\"seeds\\\"],x[\\\"perturbations\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",x[\\\"critical_path\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",x[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"][:6300],\\\"\\\\n\\\") for x in rows]'\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261119-003-r10s2 86.757 3202739 [76.343, 86.757, 86.757] [[0, 76.343, 12], [1, 86.757, 13], [2, 86.757, 13]] \n {'from': 'cpu/u_ex/reg_q.result_sel.div_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_ex/match_target_q_s0', 'slack_ns': -8.099, 'arrival_ns': 18.393, 'logic_cells': 32, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex']} \n # Bench IF successor selection\n\n`cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv` selects eight full-width next-PC sources with one masked OR. Ordinary fetch selects the held PC, PC+4, the committed BTB target, or the pending target write. Taken and fall-through recovery each select the corrected PC or its precomputed increment. The selector feeds the PC register and both synchronous replay lookups.\n\nThe committed and pending BTB tag checks feed separate target enables. The two-bit counter decisions and the ID metadata repair follow the existing training rules. Recovery takes precedence and uses instruction-bus readiness and the MEM hold to determine acceptance. Last-word parking and reset participate in the source enables. The existing FENCE.I invalidation, replay collision handling, and full-width EX validation still apply.\n\nThe timing-driven refinements qualify the ordinary paths in parallel with word availability and express the final mask as a per-bit reduction. Replay selects the requested slot bit from both bitmap sources before applying the existing same-bank bypass. The remaining hit qualifiers settle in parallel with slot selection.\n\n## Timing and throughput\n\nGowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 measured 76.343, 86.757, and 86.757 MHz. A repeat from the final worktree reproduced all three values. The 86.757 MHz median exceeds the 84.13 MHz promotion threshold. CoreMark completed 10 iterations in 3,202,739 marker-bracketed cycles with all canonical CRCs and the validation message. This projects 270.88 iterations per second, 7.87% above the champion at the same cycle count.\n\nThe two median placements are limited by the registered dependency match through the EX target comparison. Bitmap-to-replay address, divider recurrence, and re\n…[16185 of 19257 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b66abd0238cb76e5]…\n CoreMark run completed 10 iterations in 3,202,739 marker-bracketed cycles, the same as the champion. A temporary Verilator monitor counted 13,735 JALRs, 1,235 absent hints (8.99%), 747 mismatches among the 12,500 hints, and 58,391 total corrections. Zero absent hints had an actual target equal to the live fetch PC.\n- Gowin options 0, 1, and 2 reached 80.861, 80.100, and 80.100 MHz. The 80.100 MHz median yields 250.10 iterations/s, 7.67% below the champion and below the 90.75 MHz promotion threshold.\n- The first trial selected the BTB lane ahead of its ID subtractors. Option 0 reported a 12.691 ns data delay from EX's registered branch equality into an ID/EX expected operand. The final version moves lane selection after the subtractors. Its ID/EX expected operands and EX match do not appear as endpoints in the 25 reported worst setup paths for any placement. The final median is limited by replay validity feeding the next fetch PC (12.449 ns data delay). The divider result path limits option 0 (12.332 ns).\n \n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
