{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_21", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"git diff --check && sed -n '1,170p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-002-r14s1.yaml\"", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261120-002-r14s1\ntitle: \"Register fetched instructions before decode\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  Bench scores 270.88 CoreMark iter/s at 86.757 MHz in 3,202,739 timed\n  cycles. The champion's median placements limit on EX forwarding through\n  the JALR addition and successor comparison. Replay-to-register-file\n  and replay-availability-to-fetch-address paths are close behind. The\n  round-13 structural trial kept fetch and register-file reads in one\n  cycle and duplicated the reads. A registered instruction boundary can\n  shorten that path and give JALR resolution a stable younger PC to compare\n  against after the existing EX/MEM edge.\nhypothesis: |\n  Put a two-entry registered instruction queue between ordinary fetch and\n  ID. Keep the oldest word and its full PC and PC+4 in a dedicated head\n  register. Decode and read ordinary operands from that register. Capture\n  dependency matches on the edge that issues the head into ID/EX. Let the\n  producer accept from the ready bus or the tagged replay store whenever\n  there is a free slot at the start of the cycle. A full queue drains\n  before it refills, so the ordinary fetch credit comes from registered\n  occupancy and bypasses EX hold. One enqueue and one dequeue can run\n  together at occupancy one. The queue sustains one issue per cycle\n  when fed and can save ready fetches during an EX hold.\n\n  For ordinary fetch, compute the predicted successor independently of\n  queue credit and replay hit. Use acceptance to enable the PC and\n  synchronous replay read together, with redirect overriding ordinary\n  advancement. Retain the current tagged word store and banked validity.\n  Preserve the accepted word and tag as an explicit bypass when its fill\n  collides with the sampled replay address, so a held lookup can recover\n  without requiring another ready bus beat. Ready bus data retains priority.\n  Keep a same-cycle, bus-only issue path for a ready correction when MEM\n  can advance. Read the two source registers addressed by the raw bus word\n  in parallel wi\n…[2438 of 5510 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:4b03df393ac2ae5c]…\n: \"Define the registered fetch head and carry the sampled successor PC and JALR comparison enable in the EX/MEM resolution record.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Issue the registered head or the ready corrected bus word, select the matching pre-read operands at ID/EX, and capture predictor metadata and dependency matches at issue.\"\n  - file: rtl/reg_file.sv\n    description: \"Provide a second pair of combinational reads for raw bus source fields, sharing the architectural storage and applying write-first and x0 semantics on both pairs.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: \"Capture the younger candidate PC on EX advance and validate the full-width registered JALR target in recovery, preserving alignment, training, and architectural PC updates.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire separate fetch enqueue, dequeue and corrected issue handshakes, both register-file read pairs, the candidate PC, and recovery squash while preserving single-channel retirement and the external interface.\"\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
