{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_22", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nfor line in Path(\\\"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\\").open():\n    row = json.loads(line)\n    if row[\\\"id\\\"] in {\\\"hyp-20261119-002-r2s1\\\", \\\"hyp-20261119-003-r3s2\\\", \\\"hyp-20261119-002-r10s1\\\"}:\n        print(row[\\\"id\\\"], row[\\\"title\\\"], row[\\\"cycles\\\"])\n        print(row[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"][-4100:])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261119-002-r2s1 Decouple fetch with one fall-through instruction slot 4353657\n\nhyp-20261119-003-r3s2 Run the BTB ahead of decode across predicted control transfers 3959069\n# Implementation notes\n\nThe 32-entry BTB drives a request PC that follows the chosen successor on each accepted instruction response. A four-entry fall-through FIFO holds the full PC, instruction, and recorded successor. ID can bypass the empty queue or consume its fixed head while imem is unready. Load-use, dmem, and divider stalls constrain ID issue, and fetch can fill all four entries during those stalls. A full queue can dequeue and refill on the same edge.\n\nThe instruction's recorded successor goes through ID/EX. EX compares it with the full-width target and `PC + 4` in parallel with direction resolution. A mismatch or an advancing FENCE.I flushes the queue and squashes the younger ID/EX slot. RVFI reports the resolved architectural successor. At `0x000F_FFFC`, a sequential prediction holds the request and marks bit zero of the saved successor. EX's normal recovery clears the hold and supplies the full architectural address.\n\nID calculates `PC + 4` from the selected head PC and latches a one-bit FENCE.I decode for EX. Registering that decode reduced the timed instruction bits carried into EX by nine. BTB tag and counter metadata have a separate read by the head PC, including the same-index EX update bypass. The target array is read by the request PC. EX qualifies training and recovery with instruction advance. FIFO occupancy determines validity after a flush, and only occupied entries shift on a dequeue.\n\nDirected cocotb coverage includes predicted target prefetch during a dmem stall, full-queue refill, a queued load-use dependency with imem unready, wrong-path store squash on dmem release, FENCE.I refetch after a code store, and an in-range redirect from the last RAM word while fetch runs ahead of a divide.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed with `-Wall`. All five cocotb runners passed, including 23 pipeline tests.\n- Lo\n…[2453 of 5525 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b60ba6d5bdbb5948]…\n instructions through startup and post-fence sweeps. They exercise matching and conflicting write collisions, refresh by ready data, immediate exclusion of stale words after FENCE.I, and a repeated fence whose restarted sweep ends while a stale read is held.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint, the RVFI channel-0 port check, cocotb, and single-channel cosim passed. Cosim covered selftest and three random traces with and without stalls, plus stalled CoreMark with canonical CRCs, no out-of-bounds accesses, and 3,202,555 marker-bracketed cycles.\n- The unchanged single-channel `formal/run_all.sh` gate passed all 53 checks on the selected implementation. The depth-20 register proof completed in about 16 minutes.\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 measured 77.239, 80.541, and 80.541 MHz, using 4,117 logic cells and seven BSRAMs. The median corresponds to 251.49 CoreMark iterations/s, up 0.15% from 251.12. The limiting path is in the divider, and the improvement falls short of the 4.6% promotion threshold.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
