{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_16", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n r=json.loads(l)\n if r['id'].endswith(('r11s0','r12s1','r14s1','r14s2','r14s0','r12s2','r13s2')):\n  print(r['id'], r['implementation_notes'], sep='\\\\n')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261119-001-r11s0\n\nhyp-20261119-002-r12s1\n\nhyp-20261119-003-r12s2\n# JALR prediction validation\n\n## Implementation\n\nEX validates the ordinary fetch candidate with four registered groups of independent carry checks. The checks require candidate bit 0 to be clear, anchor the carry into bit 1 from the two low operand bits, and check each transition through the carry into bit 31. Each transition compares the two carries after XORing both with the current immediate bit. This lets adjacent sign-extension bits cancel in the required carry expression.\n\nEX registers the direct target match and the JALR check enable with the parked-fetch qualification. It reduces the four JALR groups in the recovery cycle and combines that result with the direct target match. The comparison registers, including the fall-through match, hold during data-memory stalls. The resolution-valid pulse tracks EX advance and is suppressed during reset.\n\nThe ALU exposes the shared addition before the ADD/SUB result selection. EX masks its bit 0 to produce the full JALR target for alignment, BTB eligibility, recovery, and RVFI. The early tap removes the ADD/SUB selector from the target path and gave the highest median Fmax of the local variants.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed with `-Wall`.\n- A combinational SAT proof established equality with ordinary candidate-PC comparison against masked 32-bit addition for arbitrary operands and candidates. It included direct targets and the parked-fetch qualifier.\n- The existing cocotb suites passed. RVFI co-simulation passed for selftest and all three random programs with zero-wait buses and with randomized stalls on both buses.\n- Stalled CoreMark validated all five CRCs without an out-of-bounds access. The start and stop markers were at cycles 12,468 and 3,215,207, giving 3,202,739 timed cycles.\n- The single-retirement `formal/run_all.sh` gate passed all 53 checks on the final RTL, including the depth-20 register-consistency check. It used `wrapper_si.sv` and `checks_si.cfg` in a scratch copy. No f\n…[8140 of 11212 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:fa24a3a852093e2e]…\nacross all 13 tag bits, adjacent conditional branches, self-loops, JALR retargeting, misaligned targets, and corrections under instruction and data bus holds. A directed test also verifies that a correctly fetched taken successor leaves a different same-index entry and its direction counter intact.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint and all 146 cocotb cases across five runners passed.\n- Co-simulation passed on selftest with and without stalls. Stalled CoreMark completed in 3,202,739 bracketed cycles with the canonical CRCs and `Correct operation validated.`\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 checks with the standard single-channel wrapper and ALTOPS checks. No formal fix attempts were needed.\n- The selected block-RAM design uses the original one-hot ordinary PC selector. The Gowin flow measured 78.949, 81.195, and 81.195 MHz across placement options 0, 1, and 2. The divider limited all three placements. The 81.195 MHz median is 6.4% below the 86.757 MHz champion at the same CoreMark cycle count.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
