{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_71", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,150p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-002-r15s1.yaml\"", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261120-002-r15s1\ntitle: \"Add a fixed spill slot at EX/MEM\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  Bench spends 3,202,739 marker-bracketed cycles on CoreMark at 86.757 MHz.\n  When dmem stalls, EX/MEM holds the request and blocks even independent\n  execution. The round-11 structural design saved 98,465 cycles with three\n  completion slots, but its request-tag-to-slot-writer path capped Gowin\n  at 78.581 MHz. A single fixed spill slot can hide a useful fraction of\n  those stalls with only local ownership and forwarding control.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add one older spill register next to EX/MEM. Keep EX/MEM as the sole\n  destination for new EX results and keep the existing fast path when\n  the spill is empty and MEM can retire EX/MEM normally. If the request\n  in EX/MEM is stalled and a younger instruction can advance, transfer\n  the older packet into the spill and replace EX/MEM with the younger\n  result on the same edge. The bus must see the same request address,\n  write data and enables before and after the transfer. Once both slots\n  are occupied, only retirement of the older spill can make room. On\n  that edge, move EX/MEM into the spill and accept a new EX result into\n  EX/MEM together when they are valid.\n\n  Drive the data port from the oldest unfinished memory request using\n  the two registered packet positions. While the spill holds an\n  unfinished memory operation it owns the port through acceptance.\n  If the spill is retiring a completed result, EX/MEM can use the\n  otherwise idle port for an ordinary RAM access. Record an accepted\n  EX/MEM access as completed\n  when it moves into the spill, including its formatted load value and\n  RVFI response fields. Use a local completion bit to prevent a second\n  request. Keep request arbitration, slot credit and write enables\n  based on fixed positions and validity bits.\n\n  Match the younger EX/MEM writer, older spill writer and MEM/WB\n  in program order with youngest-writer priority. Capture comparisons\n  before EX and update them on promotion and retirement so a h\n…[2339 of 5411 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:4bc31ea424fa9ca6]…\nand capture operands from the age-ordered result lanes, including the spill.\"\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \"Manage spill capture and promotion, service the oldest unfinished memory request, record completion and retire the oldest complete packet.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Capture dependency matches for the fixed packet positions and maintain matches and saved operands as a held consumer follows a producer across slot transitions.\"\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: \"Select the youngest eligible producer using fixed-position matches and qualify a pending load with its owner and acceptance state.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Derive EX holds from slot credit and load readiness while preserving divider waits and precise squash priority.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Exercise spill promotion and simultaneous replacement under data backpressure, including bus ownership, youngest-writer priority and exactly-once store acceptance.\"\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
