{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor i,l in enumerate(open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'),1):\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d.get('id') in ['hyp-20261119-003-r10s2','hyp-20261119-003-r13s2','hyp-20261119-001-r13s0','hyp-20261119-003-r11s2','hyp-20261119-003-r9s2']:\n  for k in ('id','fitness', 'fmax_mhz','cycles','critical_path','perturbations','implementation_notes'):\n   print(k,str(d.get(k))[:2100])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "id hyp-20261119-003-r9s2\nfitness 215.39\nfmax_mhz 68.56\ncycles 3183041\ncritical_path {'from': 'cpu/u_ex/fetch_resolution.valid_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_if/replay_entry_replay_entry_0_1_s', 'slack_ns': -10.116, 'arrival_ns': 20.411, 'logic_cells': 17, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_if', 'cpu/u_id', 'cpu/u_if']}\nperturbations [[0, 66.153, 13], [1, 68.56, 12], [2, 68.56, 12]]\nimplementation_notes # Bench replay RAM\n\n`if_stage.sv` uses a synchronous 4,096 × 39-bit replay RAM indexed by\nPC[13:2]. Each word packs validity, PC[19:14], and the instruction. The\nRAM reads from the same next-PC value that the ordinary fetch register\ncaptures. The next-PC mux uses one-hot selects for the predicted target,\nfall-through, and held PC. This improved the local median from 66.274 MHz\nwith the original mux to 68.560 MHz.\n\nReset and each advancing FENCE.I block replay immediately. The shared\nwrite port then clears validity at indices 0 through 4095, with the bus\ndriving the tag and instruction on every write. Invalidation restarts the\nsweep and clears the arm even when a taken prediction coincides with it.\nImem can supply instructions throughout the sweep. Taken predictions and\nrecoveries can arm replay during the sweep, and accepted ready imem words\nresume filling it after the final write. A sampled write strobe and\n12-bit index suppress the read/write collision, including the final\nscrub write. A ready bus response still supplies the accepted instruction\nand can refresh an existing replay word.\n\nThe pipeline tests warm the replay-specific fixtures and check bus\nretirement during a scrub, reset and consecutive FENCE.I restarts with\nimem backpressure, refill and replay after the scrub, stale-word\nsuppression at the last scrub index, tag mismatches on PC[19:14], and\nsimultaneous reads and fills at matching and distinct indices.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint completed without warnings. All five cocotb suites\n  passed, including the 50 pipeline cases.\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 checks with the configured\n  single-ret\n…[9073 of 12145 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:e4293d3f2298ba70]…\nd request. A misaligned JALR traps and recovers at PC+4. Redirects, BTB training, and RVFI use the full-width architectural target adder. Direct control transfers retain full-width successor comparisons.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed with `-Wall`.\n- All 144 cocotb cases passed, including the existing parking cases. Added regressions cover both rs1 parities, signed and odd immediates, RV32 wraparound, load replay with memory stalls, pending BTB writes and tag replacements, corrected fetches, misalignment, retirement order, and wrong-path stores.\n- Selftest passed co-simulation with both ready and stalled buses. CoreMark matched the canonical CRCs.\n- A combinational SAT check using the final ID arithmetic proved that the parity-qualified operand comparisons equal the masked RV32 target comparison for every rs1 value, 12-bit immediate, and aligned BTB target.\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 riscv-formal checks on the final RTL. No fix attempts were needed.\n\n## Performance\n\n- The stalled Cor\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
