{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nrows=list(map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')))\nfor r in rows:\n if r['id'].endswith(('r10s2','r11s2','r12s2','r13s0','r13s2','r14s2')):\n  print(r['id'],r['fmax_mhz'],r['cycles'],r['critical_path']);print(r['implementation_notes'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261119-003-r10s2 86.757 3202739 {'from': 'cpu/u_ex/reg_q.result_sel.div_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_ex/match_target_q_s0', 'slack_ns': -8.099, 'arrival_ns': 18.393, 'logic_cells': 32, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex']}\n# Bench IF successor selection\n\n`cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv` selects eight full-width next-PC sources with one masked OR. Ordinary fetch selects the held PC, PC+4, the committed BTB target, or the pending target write. Taken and fall-through recovery each select the corrected PC or its precomputed increment. The selector feeds the PC register and both synchronous replay lookups.\n\nThe committed and pending BTB tag checks feed separate target enables. The two-bit counter decisions and the ID metadata repair follow the existing training rules. Recovery takes precedence and uses instruction-bus readiness and the MEM hold to determine acceptance. Last-word parking and reset participate in the source enables. The existing FENCE.I invalidation, replay collision handling, and full-width EX validation still apply.\n\nThe timing-driven refinements qualify the ordinary paths in parallel with word availability and express the final mask as a per-bit reduction. Replay selects the requested slot bit from both bitmap sources before applying the existing same-bank bypass. The remaining hit qualifiers settle in parallel with slot selection.\n\n## Timing and throughput\n\nGowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 measured 76.343, 86.757, and 86.757 MHz. A repeat from the final worktree reproduced all three values. The 86.757 MHz median exceeds the 84.13 MHz promotion threshold. CoreMark completed 10 iterations in 3,202,739 marker-bracketed cycles with all canonical CRCs and the validation message. This projects 270.88 iterations per second, 7.87% above the champion at the same cycle count.\n\nThe two median placements are limited by the registered dependency match through the EX target comparison. Bitmap-to-replay address, divider recurrence, and replay-to-register-file paths are nearly tied. Option 0 came in 10.414 MHz below th\n…[13315 of 16387 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b9099c8d26c6f8f7]…\nacross all 13 tag bits, adjacent conditional branches, self-loops, JALR retargeting, misaligned targets, and corrections under instruction and data bus holds. A directed test also verifies that a correctly fetched taken successor leaves a different same-index entry and its direction counter intact.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint and all 146 cocotb cases across five runners passed.\n- Co-simulation passed on selftest with and without stalls. Stalled CoreMark completed in 3,202,739 bracketed cycles with the canonical CRCs and `Correct operation validated.`\n- `bash formal/run_all.sh` passed all 53 checks with the standard single-channel wrapper and ALTOPS checks. No formal fix attempts were needed.\n- The selected block-RAM design uses the original one-hot ordinary PC selector. The Gowin flow measured 78.949, 81.195, and 81.195 MHz across placement options 0, 1, and 2. The divider limited all three placements. The 81.195 MHz median is 6.4% below the 86.757 MHz champion at the same CoreMark cycle count.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
