{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_12", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n r=json.loads(l)\n if r['id'].endswith(('r5s2','r11s0','r12s1','r14s1')):\n  print(r['id'], r['title']); print(r['hypothesis']['hypothesis']); print(r['implementation_notes'][:2200])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261119-003-r5s2 Validate BTB predictions across the EX/MEM boundary\nKeep the 32-entry BTB and its single-cycle prediction from the fetch PC.\nIn EX, compare the fetch PC against PC+4 and the precomputed direct\ntarget in parallel with direction resolution. On a live EX advance,\nregister the chosen static successor and its match bit, the observed\nfull fetch PC, a taken-JALR bit, and the source and outcome needed for\ntraining. The static successor is the aligned taken B/J target or\nPC+4. In the following cycle, use the registered match for the static\nsuccessor or compare the recorded fetch PC against the registered\neffective-address sum for a taken JALR, clearing bit zero of the sum.\nSelect the correct address from those two registered candidates. Check\nevery advancing instruction, including stale predictions on a\nnon-control or trapped slot. This splits the load-to-fetch path at\nEX/MEM before the prediction verdict can steer the fetch address.\n\nContinue issuing at one instruction per ready cycle when the predicted\nsuccessor is correct. On a mismatch, kill the younger EX slot before\nit can enter EX/MEM, launch the divider, or create a validation or\ntraining record. Drive the full corrected address directly to imem\nduring the validation cycle. If imem is ready and ID can accept the\ncorrected response, latch that instruction and set the request PC to\nits sequential successor. Skip the BTB lookup for this one response\nto keep the recovery address off the ordinary BTB lookup path. Compute\ncorrected PC+4 in parallel with ordinary request PC+4 and select the\nmatching PC and PC+4 for ID. If a bus stall blocks acceptance, invalidate\nID/EX and save the corrected address as the request PC. The next cycle\ncan retry that address using the normal BTB path. Keep the older\ninstruction and its RVFI retirement.\nAn older dmem stall prevents creation of a new record. A pending\nrecord is consumed once, even when its own older EX/MEM memory operation\nstalls. Preserve that operation and the held MEM/WB forwarding value.\nPark specula\n…[9982 of 13054 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:2a526d267494e1c8]…\ng and data-memory requests with\nthe surviving instruction's validity. Read the head's predictor metadata\nat issue and overlay a pending same-index update so a queued word uses\nthe current training state. Keep the corrected fetch's sequential\ncontinuation and its existing predictor metadata repair at ID/EX.\n\nRestarting ordinary issue after a correction can cost one issue cycle.\nAt the logged correction count that is about 58,400 cycles before\naccounting for the ready fetches the queue saves during EX holds.\nAdding all 58,400 cycles would raise the median promotion threshold\nfrom 90.75 to 92.4 MHz. A 94 MHz median at that cycle count would raise\nfitness by about 6%. The divider recurrence and additional register-file\nreads may limit the gain.\n\nCheck Gowin median timing, CoreMark marker cycles and canonical UART\nvalidation. Check cold redirects and FENCE.I immediately followed by\nDIV or REM, a full queue through a data stall, and replay fills that\nalias a held lookup. Run the RVFI ordering and liveness gates.\n\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
