{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_67","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,120p' cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261120-003-r15s2.yaml && for design in page13 page16 lane_share_lsu; do if test -s '/srv/hwebench/tmp/7cb5c576/8437e684/bench-predictor-r15s2.OsPptwTN'/\\\"\"'$design\".timing; then rg '\"'score Fmax' '/srv/hwebench/tmp/7cb5c576/8437e684/bench-predictor-r15s2.OsPptwTN'/\\\"\"'$design\".timing; fi; done'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261120-003-r15s2\ntitle: Split JALR target generation and prediction validation at the page boundary\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The bench champion runs CoreMark in 3,202,739 timed cycles at a median\n  86.757 MHz. Its limiting predictor path forwards rs1, adds the JALR\n  immediate, and compares the full result against the next fetch PC.\n  The measured 58,391 corrections leave limited upside from predictor\n  accuracy alone. A signed 12-bit JALR immediate can move the base address\n  by at most one 4 KiB page. That gives EX a way to check the existing\n  prediction without waiting for the upper target sum.\nhypothesis: |\n  Validate the ordinary fetch candidate for JALR in two parts. Add\n  rs1[11:0] and imm[11:0] as unsigned values and retain the carry out.\n  Compare sum bits [11:1] with fetch_pc[11:1] and require fetch_pc[0]\n  to be zero. In parallel, compare the forwarded rs1 page against\n  fetch_pc[31:12] and the two adjacent pages using 20-bit wraparound.\n  For carry equal to imm[11], the required rs1 page is the candidate\n  page. For carry one and a nonnegative immediate it is candidate page\n  minus one. For carry zero and a negative immediate it is candidate\n  page plus one. Preselect the two page-match bits for each possible\n  carry using the immediate sign. Register them, the carry, and the low\n  match on EX advance. Reduce the predicates in the existing recovery\n  beat under the parked-fetch qualifier. Direct and fall-through checks\n  keep their existing timing.\n\n  Share the low sum with architectural JALR target generation. Increment\n  and decrement the base page in parallel with the low sum. Select the\n  original page when carry equals the immediate sign, the incremented\n  page on a positive crossing, or the decremented page on a negative\n  crossing. Concatenate the selected page with the low sum and clear\n  bit zero. Use that full target for recovery, alignment, BTB training,\n  and RVFI. Keep same-cycle corrected fetch acceptance and the existing\n  predictor and replay update rules. The page comparisons cover every\n  candidate, including both directions of RV32 wraparound and targets\n  outside the predictor's cacheable range.\n\n  A SAT check proved the target and match equal to masked RV32 addition\n  and full-width candidate equality. The shared-sum scratch prototype\n  passed selftest co-simulation with and without stalls and validated\n  stalled CoreMark in 3,202,739 bracketed cycles. Gowin measured 82.169,\n  81.418, and 81.418 MHz and used 4,257 logic cells. The EX divider result\n  path limited the median, so this prototype scored 254.21 iterations/s.\n  The timing hypothesis needs a median above 90.75 MHz at the measured\n  cycle count to merit promotion. Recheck the unchanged RVFI gates and\n  all three Gowin placements if implementing this change.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: -6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: Share the offset sum between the full JALR target and the registered prediction predicates. Select the target page and validate the candidate page in parallel while preserving correction timing.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
